export const CRAWLED_EXAM_TEXT: Record<string, string> = {"exam-cardiovascular-heme":"KABARAK                         UNIVERSITY\nUNIVERSITY EXAMINATIONS\nMAIN CAMPUS\nFIRST SEMESTER, 2020/2021 ACADEMIC YEAR\nEXAMINATION FOR BACHELOR DEGREE IN PHARMACY\nPHAM 4118: CLINICAL PHARMACY IV (CARDIOVASCULAR AND\nHEMATOPOIETIC SYSTEM)\nSTREAM: Y4S1\t\t\t\t\t\t             TIME: 9:00-11:00AM\nEXAMINATION SESSION: SEPT-DEC    \t\t    DATE: 11/02/2021 \nREAD INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY\nThe examination paper has THREE (3) sections.                                   [Total 100 Marks]\nAnswer ALL questions in sections A, B and C\nSECTION A has SIXTY Multiple Choice Questions.                                        [60 Marks]\nSECTION B has FIVE Short Answer Questions.                                               [20 Marks]\nSECTION C has ONE Long Answer Question.                                                  [20 Marks]\nAnswer questions in the BOOKLET provided. Begin each of the questions in a new page of the answer booklet. \nAdhere to the University examination rules and regulations\nDo not write anything on the question paper.\nSECTION A: MULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS ANSWER ALL                  (60 MARKS)\nAnemia is best detected in the ;\nSclera\nConjunctiva\nCornea\nRetina\nWhich of the following can cause bradycardia?\nDigoxin\nPropranolol\nAtenolol\nAll of the above\nAnatomic malformations of the heart valves, myocardium, may be best detected by conducting which one of the following tests ?\nECG\nMRI\nECHO\nCXR\nThe mean arterial pressure in a patient whose blood pressure is 160/100 mmHg is\n60 mmHg\n65 mmHg\n120 mmHg\n130 mmHg\nIn classification of hypertension, a diastolic blood pressure of 104 mmHg and systolic blood pressure of 135 mmHg suggests;\nNormality\nPre-hypertension\nStage I hypertension\nStage II hypertension\nThe goal of treatment of hypertension in a diabetic patient who presents with nephropathy is to aim for blood pressure of;\nA. \u003c140/85  \nB. \u003c130/80  \nC. \u003c125/75  \nD. \u003c140/100\nCommonest cause of secondary hypertension is ;\nA. Aortic stenosis  \nB. Drugs  \nC. Renoparenchymal disease  \nD. Cushing syndrome\nThe following antihypertensive agents are suitable in an asthmatic patient except ;\nA. Sodium nitroprusside  \nB. Labetalol \nC. Hydralazine  \nD. None of the above\nDrug of choice for reducing the acute pulmonary edema of heart failure ; \nA. Loop diuretics  \nB. Thiazide diuretics  \nC. Potassium-sparing diuretics  \nD. Osmotic diuretics\nAdverse effects of thiazide diuretics include the following except ; \nA. Hyperuricemia   \nB. Hypoglycemia  \nC. Hyperlipidemia  \nD. Hyperglycemia\nAngiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors are contraindicated in all the following except;\n                  A. Aortic stenosis  \nB. Renal aortic stenosis  \nC. First trimester of pregnancy \nD. Asthma\nAcebutolol  ;\nA. Is a cardio selective beta blocker  \nB. Is a non-selective beta blocker \nC. Is a beta blocker with vasodilatory properties \nD. None of the above \nWhich one of the following treatments are most suitable in the management of Congestive Cardiac failure?  \nA. ACEI and furosemide \nB. A beta blocker and a thiazide diuretic \nC. A beta blocker and an ACEI \nD. Spironolactone and a nitrate\nToxic effects of calcium channel blockers include  \nA. Tachycardia \nB. Atrioventricular block and congestive heart failure  \nC. Hyperkalemia \nD. Hyperglycemia\nReflex tachycardia is seen with the use of the following medication, except  \nA. Hydralazine  \nB. Prazosin  \nC. Propranolol  \nD. Minoxidil\nPreferred medication for severe hypertension in pregnancy  \nA. Methyldopa  \nB. Hydralazine  \nC. Nifedipine  \nD. Enalapril\nRegarding hypertensive crisis  \nA. Urgencies are associated with end organ damage  \nB. Immediate blood pressure reduction is recommended in hypertensive urgencies \nC. Hypertensive emergency is managed using IV therapy  \nD. Immediate-release nifedipine should be used\nThe following is untrue of dyslipidemias  \nA. Diet and genetics play a role  \nB. They are major players in atherosclerosis and plaque formation \nC. Cholesterol screening should start as soon as cardiovascular disorders occur  \nD. Pediatric screening is largely based on genetics and risk factors\nFamilial hypercholesterolaemia  \nA. Patients have normally functioning LDL receptors  \nB. Is associated with Apolipoprotein B-100 defect  \nC. Results from mutation in the gene coding for the LDL receptor  \nD. Is associated with Apolipoprotein E\nThe following drugs are associated with hyperlipidemia, except  \nA. Cyclosporine  \nB. Protease Inhibitors  \nC. Prazosin  \nD. Isotretinoin \nHDL levels can be increased in the following situations, except  \nA. Exercise  \nB. Use of ezetimibe  \nC. Use of niacin  \nD. Diets high in polyunsaturated fatty acids\nHMG-CoA reductase inhibitors\nBind intestinal bile acids\nAre the most potent and among best tolerated\nCan be used in pregnancy\nAdverse effects include flushing and pruritus\nWhich of the following statements are true of angina \nA. Prinzmetal’s angina occurs at rest, especially in the early morning hours  \nB. Stable angina occurs at rest  \nC. Symptoms of unstable angina are relieved by rest  \nD. Stable angina requires hospital admission and aggressive therapy\nThe following drugs are used to prevent recurrent ischemic symptoms EXCEPT?\nCalcium channel blockers\nBeta blockers\nThiazide diuretics\nNitrates\nThe following statements are true regarding the clinical management of ischemic heart disease EXCEPT?\nPharmacotherapy targets a reduction in cardiac workload\nStatins should only be used in the management of patients with elevated LDL levels\nACEIs have shown benefit in ischemic heart disease combined with hypertension\nControl of modifiable risk factors is important in the management of ischemic heart disease\nThe following statements are true regarding the use of antiplatelet drugs in the management of angina EXCEPT:\nAspirin has been shown to reduce the risk of major adverse cardiac events\nAspirin doses greater than 162 mg have therapeutic benefit\nClopidogrel is preferred in aspirin allergy or intolerance\nCombination of both aspirin and clopidogrel is recommended in patients with stents to prevent in-stent thrombosis\nThe following statements are true regarding the use of short acting nitrates in angina\nThey have a delayed onset of action\nThey can be used as maintenance therapy in chronic stable angina\nAdverse effects include lightheadedness\nThey can be used in combination with phosphodiesterase enzyme inhibitors \nThe following statements are true regarding the use of Calcium channel blockers in angina EXCEPT:\nThey can be used with beta blockers when initial treatment is unsuccessful\nThey are recommended when beta blockers are contraindicated\nCombination of dihydropyridines and beta blockers may improve symptoms\nNone of the above\nSymptoms of left heart failure include the following, except  \nFatigue  \nNocturia  \nDyspnea  \nCough\nHepatomegally in congestive cardiac failure is a sign of \nLeft heart failure\nRight heart failure\nBoth A and B\nAortic incompetence\nWhich one of the following is/are clinical manifestations of Left heart failure?  \nOedema  \nDyspnoea  \nC, Elevated jugular venous pressure  \nA and C\nPrecipitating factors for heart failure include the following, except  \nA. Anemia  \nB. Pregnancy  \nC. Well controlled hypertension  \nD. Use of doxorubicin\nThe following agent can be used in combination with loop diuretics in the management of heart failure:\nChlorthalidone\nEthacrynic acid\nMetolazone\nBumetanide\nWhich one of the following is a beneficial effect of spironolactone in management of heart failure?   \nIt lowers the heart rate  \nB. It increases potassium levels  \nC. It prevents ventricular remodeling  \nD. It increases blood volume\nThe following are effects of angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors in heart failure EXCEPT:\nSlow down ventricular remodeling\nLower the heart rate\nPrevent myocardial fibrosis\nLower the peripheral resistance\nThe following are beneficial effects of beta blockers in heart failure EXCEPT:\nReversing left ventricular remodeling\nPromoting plasma renin release\nImproving left ventricular systolic function\nDecreasing the heart rate\nRegarding myocardial infarction,  \nNSTEMI involves the entire thickness of the      myocardial wall  \nPremenopausal women are at higher risk  \nSTEMI involves the entire thickness of the myocardial wall   \nNone of the above is true\nRegarding cardiac biomarkers  \nMyoglobin is highly specific for cardiac injury  \nTroponin is highly specific for MI  \nCK-MB is more reliable than troponin.  \nNone of the above \nKL is a 75-year-old man who was brought to an emergency room with signs/symptoms of a MI. His blood pressure on presentation is 200/105 mm Hg, and it has been approximately 10 hours since these signs/symptoms began. If moderate to severe chest pain had been part of KL\u0027s presentation, which of the following would be correct?\nA) KL is not having an MI. \nB) The pain could be relieved with a single dose of ASA 325 mg.\nC) The pain could bemanaged with morphine sulfate 2-5 mg IV q5-15min.\nD) There is no need to treat pain associated with MI.\nIt is subsequently decided to give KL nitroglycerin (NTG) for management of his acute MI. Which of the following correctly describes use of NTG in the management of MI?\nA) NTG iscontraindicated in the management of MI\nB) Use of sublingual NTG in particular is contraindicated in the management of MI\nC) Give a single sublingual dose only on admission to the emergency department\nD) Give intravenous NTG beginning with 10-20 µg/min \nShould KL receive metoprolol as part of the management of his MI\nA) Yes; it is useful for minimizing signs/symptoms of ischemia  \nB)  No; it is contraindicated because he is receiving nitroglycerin\nC) No; it is used only if the patient had received tissue plasminogen\nD) No; it is contraindicated because his resting heart rate is greater than100 beats/minute\nCauses of arrythmias include the following, except  \nA. Myocardial infarction  \nB. Thyroid disorders  \nC. Young age  \nD. Excess alcohol consumption\nThe following is a class II antiarrythmic agent  \nA. Sotalol  \nB. Procainamide  \nC. Diltiazem  \nD. Metoprolol\nYou are following a patient who is receiving chronic oral amiodarone. Because of the toxicities of amiodarone, which of the following tests would you monitor? \nA. Chest x-ray\nB. Thyroid function tests\nC) Liver function tests\nD) Ophthalmic examinations\n45. Which of the following properties does propafenone possess? \nVaughan-Williams type Ib only  \nVaughan-Williams types Ic and II  \nVaughan-Williams type III  \nVaughan-Williams types Ia and IV\n46. The drug of choice for terminating paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia is\nLidocaine \nProcainamide \nAdenosine  \nDigoxin \n47. Anemia may present with the following signs and symptoms  \nA. Pallor  \nB. Tachycardia  \nC. Papilledema  \nD. Dizziness \n48. Iron deficiency anemia  \nA. May be associated with chronic intake of drugs causing lowered gastric PH  \nB. May lead to loss of vibration sense  \nC. May be due to chronic hemorrhage  \nD. May be due to acute hemorrhage \n49. Management of anemia of chronic disease entails  \nA. Oral iron therapy  \nB. Blood transfusion  \nC. Treating the underlying chronic condition  \nD. None of the above \n50 . Management of sideroblastic anemia is with  \nA. High doses of Vitamin C, \u003e200 mg \nB. Desferrioxamine and low doses of Vitamin C \u003c200mg,  \nC. Desferrioxamine and high doses of Vitamin C \u003e200 mg  \nD. None of the above \n51. The principal biochemical defect that leads to the development of sickle hemoglobin involves the substitution of _____________ at the sixth position of the b-polypeptide chain\nA. Phenylalanine for lysine\nB. Valine for glutamic acid\nC. Glutamic acid for alanine\nD. Proline for cysteine\n52. The principal goal of hydroxyurea therapy is to:  \nA. Decrease the amount of circulating S hemoglobin  \nB. Increase polymerization of S hemoglobin \nC. Decrease the amount of circulating F hemoglobin  \nD. Increase the adhesive properties of red cell \n53.  In the management of hemophilia  \nA. Immunomodulators have not shown benefit  \nB. Vaccination of hepatitis A and B is done regularly  \nC. Recombinant factors are cheaper than plasma derived concentrates  \nD. None of the above \n54. Disseminated intravascular coagulation is managed using the following medication, except \nA. Dermatan sulfate  \nB. Lepirudin  \nC. Warfarin  \nD. Heparin  \n55. Which of the following is true regarding prophylaxis of venous thromboembolism  \nA. Low-dose unfractionated heparin (UFH) provide improved protection compared to low molecular weight heparins (LMWHs)  \nB. Ambulation as soon as possible following surgery has been seen to significantly lower risk of venous thromboembolism ( VTE)  \nC. For hospitalized general and surgical patients, evidence supports use of fondaparinux 2.5 mg SC daily  \nD. None of the above \n56. Graduate compression stockings are  \nA. Not well tolerated by the majority of patients   \nB. An appropriate venous thromboembolism (VTE) prevention strategy for patients at moderate risk  \nC. An expensive strategy for the prevention of venous thromboembolism (VTE)  \nD. Ineffective when used in combination with pharmacologic venous thromboembolism (VTE) prophylaxis  \n57. In the long-term management of venous thromboembolism (VTE)  \nA. Anticoagulation therapy should be continued for a minimum of 6 months  \nB. Unfractionated heparin (UFH) are safer and efficacious in cancer patients and pregnant women  \nC. Warfarin can be used alongside a parenteral agent  \nD. None of the above  \n58.  The following is true about warfarin, except  \nA. It has no effect on circulating anticoagulants that have previously been formed  \nB. It should only be administered when a systematic patient monitoring system is available  \nC. It achieves its full antithrombotic effect on administration  \nD. For general surgery procedures, prophylaxis should be discontinued when the patient can ambulate regularly \n59. Which of the signs or symptoms listed below is not consistent with the diagnosis of deep vein thrombosis (DVT)?\nA) The examiner feels a palpable cord in the right leg.\nB) The patient\u0027s left leg appears red and feels hot.\nC) The patient complains of pain in the right leg when dorsiflexing the right foot.\nD) The patient\u0027s ankles are very swollen.\n60. Regarding hemophilia \nFemale carriers have a slight bleeding tendency\nIt presents as nerve compression\nHemophilia A involves deficiency of factor IX\nHemophilia B involves deficiency of factor VIII\nSECTION B: ANSWER ALL QUESTIONS \t\t\t\t\t(20 MARKS)\nList 2 classes of drugs used in dyslipidemias, stating the mode of action of each \n       (4 Marks)\nDiscuss the management of hemolytic-uremic syndrome \t\t\t       (4 Marks)\nBriefly describe the different types of angina \t\t\t\t       (4 Marks)\nOutline the management of a patient diagnosed with iron deficiency anemia     (4 Marks)\nGive two examples of angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors; what are some of the adverse effects of this class of antihypertensives? \t\t\t                 (4 Marks)\nSECTION C: ANSWER ALL QUESTIONS (20 MARKS) \nM.O, a 65-year-old known hypertensive patient with a history of coronary artery disease and type 2 diabetes mellitus presents to hospital. His presenting complaint is a feeling of breathlessness and easy fatiguability. He reports that he experiences these symptoms when he walks for short distances, climbs stairs and when he lies down flat. He says that he has resorted to using at least 2 pillows when he goes to sleep as they offer some relief. On physical examination, you note that his ankles are swollen, to which he further informs you that he no longer wears closed shoes, as they don’t fit and he only wears open shoes. You also note slight abdominal distension, and upon the review of systems he reveals that he has difficulty finishing his meals due to a feeling of early satiety and nausea. \na) Based on this information, what diagnosis would you make?\t\t\t (2 Marks)\nb) What tests would you request for to confirm the diagnosis?\t\t\t(3 Marks)\nb) What factors would precipitate symptoms of this disease? \t\t\t(5Marks)\nc) Outline a pharmaceutical care plan for M.O\t\t\t\t\t (10 Marks)","exam-endo-onc-rheum":"    KABARAK                          UNIVERSITY\n         UNIVERSITY EXAMINATIONS\nMAIN CAMPUS\nFIRST SEMESTER, 2020/2021 ACADEMIC YEAR\nEXAMINATION FOR THE DEGREE OF BACHELOR IN PHARMACY\nPHAM 5122: CLINICAL PHARMACY VIII (ENDOCRINOLOGY, JOINT \u0026 ONCOLOGY)\nSPECIAL EXAMS\nSTREAM: Y5S1\t\t\t\t\t\t                     TIME: 9:00-11:00AM\nEXAMINATION SESSION: SEPT-DEC  \t\t                     DATE: 28/01/2021\nVENUE: UNIVERSITY DOME TENT                                    COPIES: 4 \nREAD INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY\nThe examination paper has THREE (3) sections.                                           [Total 100 Marks]\nAnswer ALL questions in sections A, B and C\nSECTION A has SIXTY Multiple Choice Questions.                                             [60 Marks]\nSECTION B has FOUR Short Answer Questions.                                                   [20 Marks]\nSECTION C has TWO Long Answer Questions.                                                     [20 Marks]\nAnswer questions in the BOOKLET provided. Begin each of the questions in section C in a new page of the answer booklet. \nAdhere to the University examination rules and regulations\nSECTION A : ANSWER ALL QUESTIONS                                                          (20 MARKS)\nFOR QUESTIONS 1-3 REFER TO THE FOLLOWING CASE\nPQ is a 75 year old patient who has just been diagnosed with hypothyroidism. Her past medical history is significant for heart failure, type 2 diabetes mellitus, anemia and chronic stable angina, all of which are well-controlled. Her medications include:\nCarvedilol 6.25 mg po bid\nFerrous fumarate 300 mg po daily\nVitamin D 1000 units po daily\nGlyburide 2.5 mg po bid\nEnalapril 10 mg po bid\nFurosemide 40 mg po daily\nNitroglycerin sublingual spray prn\nPQ should be started on a low dose of levothyroxine because of her:\na. age.\nb. gender.\nc. diabetes.\nd. metoprolol use.\n. Appropriate counselling and follow-up for PQ with initiation of levothyroxine includes all of the following, EXCEPT:\na. separate the levothyroxine dose from ferrous fumarate by several hours.\nb. take levothyroxine on a full stomach for greater absorption.\nc. physician follow-up is needed in 6-8 weeks to have her thyroid function tests repeated.\nd. closer monitoring of her angina should be done during dosage titration of levothyroxine.\nFor a patient who receives a chemotherapy regimen containing cisplatin, which of the following is a significant adverse effect of cisplatin?\na. Ototoxicity\nb. Hepatotoxicity\nc. Photosensitivity\nd. Pulmonary fibrosis\nBG, a 45 year old male with type 1 diabetes mellitus, is currently using a premixed 30/70 combination of regular and intermediate-acting insulin subcut bid (before breakfast and supper). The following blood glucose values are observed:\n5.8 mmol/L at 2200 h\n3.6 mmol/L at 0300 h\n10.2 mmol/L at 0800 h (2 hours after breakfast)\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate initial adjustment for BG’s insulin regimen?\na. Decrease the suppertime insulin dose.\nb. Increase the suppertime insulin dose.\nc. Decrease the breakfast time insulin dose.\nd. Increase the breakfast time insulin dose.\nWhich of the following is/are considered Diabetes Risk Factor(s)? \nI. Physical inactivity.\nII. Women who delivered a baby greater than 9 lb.\nIII. First-degree relative with diabetes.\t\nIV. High-risk race/ethnicity\t\nV. Hypertension ≥ 140/90 mm Hg\nI, III, IV and V only\nII only\nAll \nI, III and IV only\nCC, a 72 year old female, complains to the pharmacist that her stomach has been bothering her recently. Current medications include: levothyroxine 100 mcg po daily (x 30 years), acetaminophen 500 mg po qid (x 5 months), atorvastatin 40 mg po at bedtime (x 4 years), ibuprofen 400 mg po tid prn for joint pain (x 2 months) and zopiclone 3.75 mg po at bedtime prn (x 3 months). Which of the following drug therapy problems is most likely contributing to CC’s recent symptoms?\na. Too high a dosage of atorvastatin\nb. Too high a dosage of zopiclone\nc. Use of ibuprofen without gastroprotection\nd. Drug interaction between atorvastatin and zopiclone\nJQ is a 67 year old male with type 2 diabetes that is controlled with insulin. Today, JQ’s wife calls the pharmacist to inquire what to do regarding JQ’s very low blood glucose reading (2.8 mmol/L). She also notes that he seems to be confused. JQ’s wife should be instructed to:\na. take JQ immediately to the nearest emergency department.\nb. have JQ eat a carbohydrate-rich meal and retest in 1 hour.\nc. give JQ a 15-20 gram glucose supplement and retest in 15 minutes.\nd. retest JQ’s blood glucose level in 1 hour and phone back if it remains low.\nFor questions 8-9 refer to this case\nTK is a 63 year old male with chronic kidney disease (Cr Cl = 29 mL/min/1.73m2) and gout. He experienced his last gout attack about 2 months ago. Today his toe is extremely painful, hot, red and swollen. At a walk-in clinic, he receives a prescription for naproxen 500 mg po bid for 5 days. TK’s other current medication is pravastatin 20 mg po at bedtime.\nWhat drug therapy problem should the pharmacist identify for TK?\na. Naproxen is inferior to indomethacin for the treatment of acute gout.\nb. The duration of naproxen treatment is too short.\nc. Naproxen should be avoided in patients taking pravastatin.\nd. Naproxen should be avoided in patients with renal dysfunction.\nFollowing successful resolution of the acute episode, TK’s physician decides that he should initiate allopurinol. TK should be advised to:\na. limit fluid intake.\nb. take medication on an empty stomach.\nc. use precautions to avoid photosensitivity.\nd. report any skin rash or itching to the physician\nA community pharmacist would like to begin a diabetic education program for local patients newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus. Beneficial information for this group includes all of the following EXCEPT:\na.  strategies to minimize cardiac risk factors.\nb.  facts on diabetes and its complications.\nc.  technique for the mixing of insulins\nd.  appropriate dietary recommendations.\nOne of the following would be a reason to withhold metformin therapy either temporarily or permanently \nReceipt of oral contrast material for an upper GI series\nDevelopment of advanced renal insufficiency\nOnset of a short, self-treated viral illness\nNew-onset stage I heart failure not requiring pharmacologic therapy\nThiazide diuretics should NOT be used as a first line treatment for hypertension in patient suffering from:\na. Hyperlipidemia\nb. Heart failure\nc. COPD\nd. Gout\nPatients taking sulfonylureas or meglitinides should be counseled about which of the following?\ntake medication, even if they are not able to eat\nsigns of hypoglycemia and appropriate action\nmedications may cause nausea and weight loss\ntake extra medications if glucose levels are elevated\nAmiodarone should be carefully prescribed to patients suffering from:\na. Hyperthyroidism\nb. Arrhythmia\nc. Depression\nd. Seizure\nA woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus has the following laboratory test results: HbA1c: 9% (4-6%); AST: 218 IU/L; and SCR: 2.0 mg/dL. Which of the following antidiabetic agents would you recommend for her treatment?\na. Insulin\nb. Rosiglitazone\nc. Metformin\nd. Pioglitazone\nA 27-year-old woman presents with symptoms of diarrhea, racing heart, weight loss despite increased appetite, and heat intolerance. Her laboratory tests are consistent with moderate hyperthyroidism. Which of the following would be appropriate initial treatment?\n(i) Propranolol 20 mg qid\n(ii) SSKI 10 drops tid\n(iii) Propylthiouracil 300 mg tid\n(iv) 131I 100 mCi\na. (i), (ii), and (iii) are correct\nb. (i) and (iii) are correct\nc. (ii) and (iv) are correct\nd. only (iv) is correct\nA 25-year-old pregnant woman is diagnosed with Graves\u0027 disease. What is the best management of this patient\u0027s thyroid disorder?\na. Propylthiouracil 600 mg/day\nb. 131I 5 mCi given one time as a clear, tasteless liquid\nc. Levothyroid 0.1 mg PO qd\nd. Do nothing\nIodides may be used before thyroid surgery to\na. decrease vascularity.\nb. prevent conversion of peripheral T4 to T3.\nc. Inhibit thyroid hormone synthesis.\nd. Ameliorate thyrotoxic sympthoms.\nFD, a 58 year old male with hypertension, asks the pharmacist if cranberry juice would be useful for his current symptoms, which include frequency and a large volume of urine, but no urgency, or painful urination. Further questioning reveals that for the past 2 months he has also experienced polydipsia and polyphagia. The pharmacist should refer FD to his physician because these symptoms are consistent with:\nA urinary tract infection.\nProstate hyperplasia.\nDiabetes mellitus.\nRenal complications of hypertension.\nA biological safety cabinet would be the best choice for preparing a parenteral formulation of:\nAlteplase.\nDoxorubicin.\nNitroglycerin.\nPenicillin.\nPQ is a 75 year old patient who has just been diagnosed with hypothyroidism. Her past medical history is significant for heart failure, type 2 diabetes mellitus, anemia and chronic stable angina, all of which are well-controlled. Her medications include: Carvedilol 6.25 mg po bid, Ferrous fumarate 300 mg po daily, Vitamin D 1000 units po daily, Glyburide 2.5 mg po bid, Enalapril 10 mg po bid, Furosemide 40 mg po daily, Nitroglycerin sublingual spray prn. Which of the following parameters is the most appropriate for PQ\u0027s self-evaluation of the effectiveness of levothyroxine therapy?\nIncreased energy\nWeight loss\nImproved vision\nLess frequent angina\nA community pharmacist would like to begin a diabetic education program for local patients newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus. Beneficial information for this group includes all of the following EXCEPT:\nStrategies to minimize cardiac risk factors.\nFacts on diabetes and its complications.\nUse of the various glucometers.\nTechnique for the mixing of insulins.\nA woman with a cardiac arrhythmia is being treated long-term with amiodarone. This drug can cause biochemical changes and clinical signs and symptoms that resemble those associated with which of the following endocrine diseases/disorders?\nAddisonian crisis\nHypothyroidism\nDiabetes insipidus\nDiabetes mellitus\nA recalcitrant patient with Type 2 diabetes mellitus is notoriously noncompliant with medication and diet recommendations. However, he thinks he’s smart enough to fool the physician into thinking otherwise: he takes his medication and eliminates nearly all carbohydrate intake for a few days before each clinic visit, knowing he will get a finger stick for a spot check of serum glucose levels. The simplest, most cost-effective, and most informative way for the physician to assess for past noncompliance and long-term glycemic control would be to perform or measure which of the following?\nGlucose concentration in venous blood sample\nGlucose tolerance test\nHb A1C\nSerum levels of the antidiabetic drug\nA 35-year-old woman has Graves’ disease, a small goiter, and symptoms that are deemed “mild-to-moderate.” Propylthiouracil is prescribed. Which of the following is the most serious adverse response to this drug, for which close monitoring is required?\nAgranulocytosis\nCholestatic jaundice\nGout\nRenal tubular necrosis\nA patient with a history of Type 2 diabetes mellitus presents in the emergency department. His complaints include nonspecific gastrointestinal symptoms, including nausea and vomiting. He states he is bloated and has abdominal pain. The diagnosis is lactic acidosis, and the suspicion is that it was caused by an antidiabetic drug. Which of the following drugs is this patient most likely to be taking?\nAcarbose\nGlipizide\nGlyburide\nMetformin\nA 47-year-old woman with choriocarcinoma is treated with very high doses of methotrexate (MTX). You anticipate significant host cell toxicity in response to the high MTX dose. Which of the following drugs would you give to limit toxic effects of the MTX on normal host cells?\nDeferoxamine\nLeucovorin\nN-acetylcysteine\nPenicillamine\nWe administer vincristine, the prototype of the vinca alkaloids, to a patient with a tumor that is likely to be responsive to this drug. Which of the following is the most likely adverse response to this drug?\nNephrotoxicity, renal dysfunction or failure\nNeutropenia\nPeripheral sensory and motor neuropathy\nPulmonary damage\nA cancer patient develops severe irreversible cardiomyopathy because the maximum lifetime dose of an anticancer drug was exceeded. Which of the following is most likely responsible for this patient’s symptoms?\nBleomycin\nCisplatin\nCyclophosphamide\nDoxorubicin\nA 41-year-old woman is admitted to the outpatient area of the hematology-oncology center for her first course of adjuvant chemotherapy for metastatic breast cancer following a left modified radical mastectomy and axillary lymph node dissection for infiltrating ductal carcinoma of the breast. Two biopsies were positive for cancer. Following premedication with dexamethasone and ondansetron, she will receive combination chemotherapy with doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, and fluorouracil. Pre-medications include intravenous ondansetron and dexamethasone. Twenty-four hours after the first course of chemotherapy, she will start a 10-day regimen with filgrastim. Which of the following is the most likely reason for administering the filgrastim?\nControl of nausea and emesis\nPotentiate the anticancer effects of the chemotherapeutic agents\nPrevent doxorubicin-induced cardiotoxicity\nReduce the risk/severity of chemo-induced neutropenia and related infections\nA cancer patient receives prophylactic allopurinol before a course of chemotherapy. Which of the following is the main reason for which the allopurinol is given?\nFacilitate host cell detoxification of the chemotherapeutic drug, thereby reducing host cell toxicities\nInhibit the potential for DNA repair, by topoisomerases, that otherwise might lead to chemotherapy failure\nPrevent myelosuppression and related blood dyscrasias\nReduce the risk of hyperuricemia and its main consequences (renal damage, gout) that can occur with a massive cell kill\nAs part of a comprehensive cancer chemotherapy regimen for metastatic breast carcinoma, we treat our patient with ondansetron. Which of the following is the most likely purpose for administering this drug?\nSuppress chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting\nBlock estrogen receptors, thereby enhancing the efficacy of the cyclophosphamide\nPrevent cardiotoxicity caused by one of the anticancer drugs in the combination\nPrevent metabolism of adrenal cortical androgens to estrogens, which would facilitate breast tumor growth\nA 25-year-old woman presents with insomnia and fears she may have “something wrong with her heart.” She describes “her heart jumping out of her chest.” She feels healthy otherwise and reports she has lots of energy. Lab tests confirm hyperthyroidism. Which of the following is a drug that produces a permanent reduction in thyroid activity?\nRadioactive iodine\nMethimazole\nPropylthiouracil\nThiocyanate (SCN–)\nThe most reliable guide to adjustment of thyroxine dose in a patient of hypothyroidism is:\nPulse rate\nBody weight\nSerum thyroxine level\nSerum TSH level\nA major disadvantage of the use of sulfonylureas in patients with diabetes is:\nRisk of hypoglycaemia\nVitamin B12 malabsorption\nWeight loss\nTreatment cost\nIn the management of type 2 diabetes mellitus, which of the following is not a rational consideration for drug choice:\nPatient characteristics and preferences\nEfficacy and durability of glucose-lowering effect\nColour of the pill\nPotential for cardiovascular and renal benefits\nThe risk factors for type 2 diabetes mellitus include:\nfamily history\nbeing overweight\nhigh intake of dietary fat\nAll of the options listed are correct\nWhich statement best describes the differences between the characteristics of type 1 and type 2 diabetes:\npersons with type 2 diabetes usually require lower doses of insulin than person with type 1 diabetes because they have a milder form of diabetes\npersons with type 1 diabetes rapidly develop chronic complications\nautoimmune factors are involved in the pathogenesis of type 1 but not type 2 diabetes\npersons with type 1 diabetes can increase endogenous insulin production by taking oral hypoglycemic agents\nAccording to trials on diabetes prevention, high-risk individuals can reduce their risk to develop diabetes by doing the following:\nEating a very low carbohydrate diet\nConsuming a diet high in monounsaturated fats\nLosing 5-7% of body weight through a hypocaloric low-fat diet and 30 minutes of daily activity\nInitiating metformin 850 mg BID and practicing daily vigorous exercise\nWhich of the following are tests for the diagnosis for diabetes:\nSymptoms and a random blood sugar over 180 mg/dl (10 mmol/L)\nFasting blood sugar over 126mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L), repeated and confirmed on a separate day\n2-hour blood glucose in oral glucose tolerance test of over 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L)\nAll the mentioned tests can be used\nAll the following symptoms/signs are included in the clinical features of diabetic ketoacidosis EXCEPT: \nLower back pain\nTachypnea and tachycardia\nAbdominal pain and vomiting\nCheyne – Stokes respiration\nWhat is the first step in the management of diabetic ketoacidosis?\nTo provide fluids intravenously\nTo provide insulin\nTo provide bicarbonate\nTo initiate insulin and fluids simultaneously\nWhich of the following are examples of autonomic neuropathy?\nexcess perspiration on lower extremities and gastroparesis\nfoot ulcers and skin infections\ngastroparesis and silent MI\ndecreased taste and smell sensation\nA 77 - year - old diabetic patient is being treated for HHS with continuous IV insulin infusion of 7 IU/h and 0.9% normal saline at a rate of 250 ml/h. A few hours later he is found to have a plasma glucose level of 58 mg/dl (3.2 mmol/L). He is started on 5% dextrose at a rate of 250 ml/h. Which of the following is an appropriate next response?\nDecrease the insulin infusion rate to 4 IU/h\nIncrease the insulin infusion rate to 10 IU/h to compensate for the intravenous glucose given\nStop the insulin infusion and start subcutaneous insulin\nContinue the IV insulin infusion at 7 IU/h\nWhich of the following statements is true?\nThe mortality rate in Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) is around 1%\nMyocardial infarction can be a precipitating event for HHS to develop\nPatients with HHS usually have normal renal function\nHyperglycemia in HHS is usually due to excessive oral carbohydrate intake\nWhich of the following statements is false?\nAltered mental status is the most common reason patients with HHS are brought to the hospital\nIn a comatose diabetic patient with an effective serum osmolality \u003c 320 mOsm/kg, another cause of the impaired mental state should be sought\nPatients with HHS usually have an increased anion gap metabolic acidosis\nInsulin therapy lowers the K + concentration and may cause severe hypokalemia\nALL the following are predisposing factor for hypoglycemia in insulin - treated patients EXCEPT? \nRenal failure\nOlder age\nAlcohol ingestion\nConcomitant use of Chlorthalidone \nWhich of the following sentences is correct?\nMetformin is contraindicated in all patients with a serum creatinine \u003e 1.4 mg/dl (123.8 μ mol/L)\nMetformin can be used safely in patients hospitalized for heart failure\nMetformin is contraindicated in patients with chronic stable angina\nMetformin is contraindicated in patients who have GFR\u003c 30 ml/min per 1.73 m 2\nWhat is correct concerning cerebral edema in DKA?\nA patient with cerebral edema will present with tachycardia\nCerebral edema never supervenes before the initiation of fluid administration for the treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis\nThe treatment of cerebral edema is discontinuation of insulin administration\nCerebral edema is more frequent in younger patients with newly onset Type 1 diabetes\nA 52-year-old woman presented with intense pain, warmth, and redness in the first toe on her left foot. Examination of fluid withdrawn from the inflamed joint revealed crystals of uric acid. In the treatment of this woman’s acute attack of gout, a high dose of colchicine will reduce the pain and inflammation. However, many physicians prefer to treat acute gout with a corticosteroid or indomethacin because high doses of colchicine are likely to cause:\nBehavioral changes that include psychosis\nHigh blood pressure\nRash\nSevere diarrhoea\nWhich of the following is an appropriate initial dose regimen for methotrexate in an adult with rheumatoid arthritis?\n50 mg orally daily\n50 mg orally once weekly\n7.5 mg orally daily\n7.5 mg orally once weekly\nHydroxychloroquine monitoring for toxicity symptoms would include\nIncreased frequency of urination.\nVisual changes.\nDry mouth.\nDrowsiness.\nWhich of the viruses below DO NOT cause cancer as a result of chronic infection?\nHerpes simplex viruses (HSV)\nHuman papilloma virus (HPV)\nHepatitis B virus (HBV)\nEpstein-Barr virus\nWhich of the following patients is at highest risk for cervical cancer?\nNone of the patients are at risk for cervical cancer.\nA 32 year old in a monogamous relationship who declined the HPV vaccine.\nA 21 year old who reports first sexual partner at the age of 14 and that she has had at least 10 sex partners.\nA 60 year old with a history of syphilis and cigarette smoking.\nWhich of the following is a hormonal agent used as a chemotherapy of breast cancer?\nTamoxifen citrate\nIfosfamide\nCarmustine\nMethotrexate\nThe wasting syndrome associated with cancer is termed___________\nAchalasia\nCachexia\nAtelexis\nCacoguesia\nOne of the most common forms of leukaemia is Chronic Myelogenous Leukaemia (CML). In most cases these leukemic cells contain a chromosomal abnormality that is a \"target\" for drug therapy that is not seen in non-leukemic white blood cells. A current treatment of choice for this form of cancer is:\nbleomycin\nimatinib\nmechlorethamine\ntamoxifen\nIntrathecal therapy of methotrexate is given to children with ALL for one of the following reasons\nCNS Prophylaxis\nIncrease the systemic levels\nReduce toxicity\nEnhance compliance\nA patient being treated with the ABVD drug regimen for Hodgkin\u0027s disease presents to the Emergency Department with the chief complaint of shortness of breath upon exertion \u0026 a nagging dry cough. A chest Xray indicates the presence of pulmonary infiltration. Which of the drugs in this patient\u0027s regimen is the most likely cause \u0026 should be discontinued?\nBleomycin\nDacarbazine\nDoxorubicin\nVinblastine\nA 43-year-old man with Hodgkin\u0027s disease was treated with the MOPP drug regimen (merchlorethamine + vincristine + procarbazine + prednisone) followed by ABVD (Adriamycin + bleomycin + vinblastine + dacarbazine). Four years later he develops a cardiomyopathy, followed by CHF. The drug in his treatment regimen that was most likely responsible for causing this is:\nAdriamycin (doxorubicin)\nBleomycin\nDacarbazine\nMerchlorethamine\nSECTION B: ANSWER ALL QUESTIONS                                                                          (20 MARKS)\nA 72-year-old woman who lives aloneattends the surgery complaining ofnocturia, thirst and weight loss. She hashad diabetes for 12 years. She has a BMIof 24.8 kg/m2. She is currently treated withmetformin, tolbutamide and pioglitazoneat maximally tolerated doses. Her HbA1c is10.2% (88 mmol/mol). She is reluctant tostart insulin but wants to feel better.\nIdentify two treatment goals for this patient                                                            (2 marks)\nExplain how you would escalate treatment in this patient as per AACE treatment algorithm \n                                                                                                                                 (2marks)\nElaborate on 3 patient counselling points regarding the new medication that this patient requires  \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(3 marks) \nJ.K is a 68-year-old farmer who presents to the ED with chief complaints of unintentional weight loss, heat intolerance and tachycardia. The physician requests for thyroid function tests and a diagnosis of primary hyperthyroidism is made. J.K has been on follow up for congestive heart failure for the last five years. Her current medication include P.O furosemide 40mg OD and Lisinopril 10mg OD. \n(a) Elaborate three causes of primary hyperthyroidism \t\t\t\t\t(3 marks)\n(b) Describe two parameters that should be monitored while treating J.K’s hypothyroidism.                                                                                 \n                                                                                                                                               (2 Marks)\nT.M is 53 year old lady who has been working as a hawker for the last 20 years. She presents with 9 weeks history of joint pains and stiffness.\nAs the pharmacist developing her pharmaceutical care plan:\nList four questions that you would ask about her joint pain and stiffness? \n                                                                                                                                         (2 Marks)\n(b) The attending medical officer requests for several Tests. A diagnosis of rheumatoid arthritis is made. Propose a pharmaceutical care plan for the management of T.M.                              (4 Marks)\n(a) Identify one regimen used for the management of Hodgkin lymphoma                         (2 Marks)\nSECTION C: ANSWER ANY TWO QUESTIONS                                                               (40 MARKS)\nA.M is a 24 year old patient admitted to the HDU at PGH Nakuru. His clinical profile is as follows:\nCC: Polyuria, polydipsia, lower abdominal pain for 2/52\nPMH: Insignificant\nO/E: Sick looking, temperature 400c, not jaundiced, not anemic, sunken eyes, reduced skin turgor\nCVS: BP 88/56 mmHg, PR 100, normal but feeble heart sounds\nR/S: RR 25/ min, aromatic oduor, no added breath sounds\nGIT: abdominal pain\nN/S: Disoriented in time, place and person (confused)\nLABORATORY RESULTS:\nRBS 24 mmol/L  N (3-6)\nK+   4.5 Meq        N (3.5-5.5)\nNa+ 143 Meq       N (135-145)\nPH    7.1              N (7.35-7.45)\nAnion Gap 18      N ( \u003c12)\nHCO3-       16      N (22-26)\nIdentify the most likely diagnosis                                                                               (1 Mark)\nSuggest two reasons for your answer in (i) above                                                     (2 Marks)\nUsing the information provided, identify the most likely trigger for this condition   (1 Mark)\nDescribe the stepwise acute management for this patient                                         (6 Marks)\nElucidate the treatment and prevention of the following toxicities associated with cancer chemotherapy:\nFebrile neutropenia                                                                                                  (3 Marks)\nChemotherapy induced Nausea and vomiting (for highly emetogenic and moderately emetogenic regimens)                                                                                                (4 Marks)\nExtravasation of:\nPaclitaxel                                                 \t\t\t\t\t(1 mark)\nNitrogen Mustard\t\t\t\t\t\t                    \t(1 mark)\nMitomycin \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(1 mark)","exam-hospital-practice":"                          KABARAK           UNIVERSITY\nUNIVERSITY EXAMINATIONS\nMAIN CAMPUS\nSECOND SEMESTER 2020/2021 ACADEMIC YEAR\nEXAMINATION FOR THE DEGREE OF BACHELOR OF PHARMACY\nPHAM 5325: CLINICAL PHARMACY XI\nSTREAM:\tY5/S3\t - REGULAR\t\t\t      TIME: 9:00-11:00AM\nEXAMINATION SESSION: MAY-AUGUST 2021   \tDATE: 17/08/2021\nREAD INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY\n1.\tThe exaMination paper has THREE (3) sections.         [Total 100 Marks]\nAnswer ALL questions in sections A, B and C\n2.\tSECTION A has THIRTY Multiple Choice Questions.   \t [30 Marks]\n3.\tSECTION B has SEVEN Short Answer Questions.   \t\t [40 Marks]\n4.\tSECTION C has Two Long Answer Questions.\t\t\t [30 Marks]\n5.\tAnswer questions in the BOOKLET provided\n6.Do not write anything on the question paper\n7.Adhere to the University exaMination rules and regulations\nSECTION A: MULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS            (30 MARKS)\nThe priMe responsibility of a hospital pharMacist is \nEnsuring availability of Medicines \nCoMpounding \nPrescribing \nDrug safety\nWhich of the following stateMents is true regarding the Medicines and therapeutics coMMittee \nIt influences iMpleMentation of evidenced based care in the hospital \nAll hospital pharMacists are MeMbers \nIt is tasked with drug therapy Monitoring in the hospital \nIt iMpleMents the policies and philosophies of the governing board \nResponsibility of a pharMacist in direct patient care includes \nChecking for accuracy of doses prepared \nPeriodical review of treatMent charts to ensure all doses are adMinistered \nEnsuring that established policies and procedures in the hospital are followed \nMaintaining professional coMpetence, particularly in knowledge of drug stability and incoMpatibilities\nMedication review entails \nAbility to carry out research for iMproveMent of patient care \nAbility to estiMate Medication needs of the facility to MiniMize stockouts \nAbility to ensure good patients understanding of treatMent \nProvision of Medical care pursuit to the objectives and policies of the hospital.\nDifferences between Institutional pharMacy practice and coMMunity pharMacy practice includes \nProvision of counselling services\nProvision of patient education \nPhysician-Patient-PharMacist-Nurse relationship \nResearch to iMprove service delivery\nEleMents of rational prescribing include \nShortest duration of treatMent to encourage coMpliance and MiniMize risk   \nExpert opinion is preferred over current evidence \nPrescribed treatMent is Mandatory for the patient \nTakes into account health seeking behavior of the patient\nThe following stateMents are true of pharMacy-based screening, except \nThe cost is cheaper \nProper screening records are Maintained \nEvaluation of tests for validity is done \nEasy accessibility\nMedication errors include \nDrug tolerance \nWrong doses \nAdverse drug reactions \nPotential risk\nWhen prescribing ……………  naMe of drug Must be used \nProprietary \nNon-Proprietary \nBrand \nCheMical \nThe following stateMents are true of pharMacy records, except \nCharges of drugs to patients are included \nThey are useful when screening for drug allergies \nThey provide data for use in research and education \nThey include the drug kardex\nThe need of a hospital forMulary systeM is increasing because of \nIncrease in the nuMber of drugs in the Market \nIncreased influence of Marketing by Manufacturers \nCoMpetition in Marketing practice \nIt is a requireMent by the regulatory body\nIdentify a Medical device correctly Matched with its function \nPatella haMMer – Detecting the consensual reflex \nPaceMakers – For detecting and recording the electrical activity in the heart\nElectronic pulse Machines – ManageMent of acute pain through patient-controlled analgesia\nGiving sets – AdMinistration of fluids and blood\nIn…...SysteM both pharMacy and nursing station are responsible for drug distribution \nFloor stock systeM \nUnit dose systeM \nCoMbination systeM \nIndividual prescription order systeM\nA Satellite pharMacy in a hospital is located in\nEach floor \nAt a central location in the hospital \nThe sterile processing unit \nDepends on hospital type\nThe following stateMents regarding vaccines are true, except \nThe Kenyan schedule vaccinates children at birth, 2,4,6 and at 12 Months \nPharMacists are authorized to prescribe and adMinister vaccines \nVaccines are routinely adMinistered in the deltoid Muscle, as opposed to gluteal Muscle \nStorage conditions of vaccines like Oral Polio Vaccine (OPV) entails freezing at -20°C\nWhich of the following is true of patient counselling \nIt is synonyMous with patient education \nIt is preventive in nature \nIt is applicable to prescription and non-prescription Medication \nIt is often docuMented as required  \nWith regard to prescription processing, which of the following is false \nPrescriptions should be stored for at least 2 years in the pharMacy preMises\nIt is the responsibility of qualified pharMacists and technicians which cannot be delegated \nWhen the person handling it is not a pharMacist, self-checking for accuracy Must be done \nRecord keeping is a legal and professional requireMent\nThe Most specific \u0026 sensitive Method for assessMent of coMpliance can be used to detect potent therapeutic agent in body fluids is \nDrug analysis \nHistory taking \nResidual Tablet counting \nPharMacy records\nHow are prescription Medicines different froM over the counter (OTC) ones? \nThey contain high concentration of active ingredients \nThey do not contain preservatives \nThey are unsafe for use without Medical supervision \nThey are prone to drug interactions\nIn coMModity ManageMent, lead tiMe refers to \nThe duration it takes to utilize available stocks \nA period when deMand for stock is low \nTiMe taken to consuMe half the stock \nTiMe between Making an order and receiving the goods  \nThe generic equivalent of a drug contains \nDifferent active coMponent of the saMe class \nSoMe active coMponents contained in the branded drug \nThey have siMilar pharMacokinetic and pharMacodynaMic profiles \nNone of the above\nThe following stateMents is true of drug therapy Monitoring, except \nInvolves assessing for appropriateness of use of generics \nIt is the responsibility of nurses \nAlways ensures adherence to forMulary Medicines \nChecks for therapeutic duplication\nWhich of the following tools is suitable in detecting anatoMical MalforMations of heart valves \nMRI \nChest X-ray \nElectrocardiograph \nEchocardiography\nWhich of the following paraMeters is the Most reliable when calculating pediatric doses?\nHeight \nBody weight \nSurface area \nAge\nWhich of the following explains purposes of using a stethoscope \nTo ascertain character of breath sounds \nTo detect any added sound \nTo deterMine whether breath sounds are equal on both sides \nAll the above\nWhich one of the following is not a coMponent of a pharMaceutical consultation? \nMonitoring of drug therapy \nCounselling the patient on drug use \nProcuring drugs froM the drug store \nTaking a patient\u0027s Medication history\nWhich of the following patients are at the highest risk of suffering froM an adverse drug reaction? \nAn 8-Month-old infant receiving a prescription for an antibiotic \nA 22-year-old patient with asthMa receiving prescriptions for inhalers to relieve and prevent their asthMa \nA 48-year-old patient who has hypertension and receives a prescription for an ACE Inhibitor D.  A 68-year-old patient who has oedeMa receiving a prescription for a diuretic.\nA 40-year-old Man who has been buying analgesics froM your pharMacy coMes to you coMplaining of upper abdoMinal pain and an episode of voMiting for the last three days. You notice that his eyes are yellow. What would be the Most appropriate course of action?\nEvaluate his condition and prescribe Medication to relieve syMptoMs \nIdentify the drug causing his syMptoMs and withdraw/substitute it \nRefer hiM for further Medical assessMent \nAdvise hiM to stop using the analgesics\nThe role of the pharMacist in selection of therapeutic alternatives requires which of the following clinical skills \nperforMance of drug utilization evaluation \nAbility to counsel patients on drug adherence and coMpliance \nReview and critique drug literature \nAll the above\nRegarding preparation of cytotoxic drugs, the following is true, except \nIt is done aseptically \nThey require cold chain storage conditions \nincludes careful segregation of cheMotherapeutic waste \nPersonal protective equipMent is required\nSECTION B: SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS                               (40 MARKS)\nState five purposes of Medical record keeping                                                            (5Marks)\nJ.N. a newly diagnosed diabetes patient coMes to your coMMunity and you prescribe an interMediate insulin e.g., Mixtard. To facilitate drug therapy Monitoring, you recoMMend daily blood glucose Monitoring. Two weeks later, she presents to your pharMacy with records showing elevated blood glucose readings. What are the possible causes of this scenario, and what action should be taken?                                                                 (5 Marks) \nOutline five coMMon Mistakes during prescription processing                                 (5 Marks)\nDescribe any five-routine pharMacy-based screening tests                                         (5 Marks)\nWhy is it iMportant for a pharMacist to be well versed with poison inforMation?    (4 Marks)\nState the use of each of the following Medical devices \nCatheters                                                                                                                 (2 Marks)\nOsMotics                                                                                                                (2 Marks)\nDialysis Machine                                                                                                    (2 Marks)\nDiscussfive ethical issues relating to the provision of drug information                   (10 Marks)\nSECTION C: LONG ANSWER QUESTIONS                                  (30 MARKS)\nDiscuss the following aspects of institutional pharMacy \nEssential functions of a Hospital PharMacy(5 Marks) \nManageMent of Controlled Drugs (5 Marks) \nQuality assurance and quality control of Medicines (5 Marks)\nWith regard to coMMunity pharMacy practice, discuss the following \nResponsibilities of a pharMacist (10 Marks)  \nConsultation styles (5 Marks) ","exam-antimicrobials":"KABARAK                            UNIVERSITY\nUNIVERSITY EXAMINATIONS\nMAIN CAMPUS\nFIRST SEMESTER, 2020/2021 ACADEMIC YEAR\nEXAMINATION FOR BACHELOR DEGREE IN PHARMACY\nPHAM 4117: CLINICAL PHARMACY III (PRINCIPLES OF \nANTIMICROBIAL THERAPY)\nSTREAM: Y4S1\t\t\t\t\t\t\t   TIME: 9:00-11:00AM\nEXAMINATION SESSION: SEPT-DEC   \t\t    DATE: 05/02/2021 \nREAD INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY\nThe examination paper has THREE (3) sections.                                [Total 100 Marks]\nAnswer ALL questions in sections A\u0026B and ONLY one in  C\nSECTION A has SIXTY Multiple Choice Questions.                                       [60 Marks]\nSECTION B has FOUR Short Answer Questions.                                             [20 Marks]\nSECTION C has TWO Long Answer Questions.                                                [20 Marks]\nAnswer questions in the BOOKLET provided. Begin each of the questions in a new page of the answer booklet. \nAdhere to the University examination rules and regulations\nDo not write anything on the question paper.\nSECTION A: ANSWER ALL THE QUESTIONS (60 marks)\nSelection of presumptive antimicrobial therapy is based on all the following except?\nLocal susceptibility data.\nNational susceptibility data.\nPatient host factors\nDrug factors\nThe utility of an antibiogram is that it shows antimicrobial susceptibility trends\nOver a large geographic area.\nFor an individual patient.\nIn research laboratory strains.\nIn a localized population.\nWhich of the following is a possible reason for antimicrobial failure?\nPatient experienced drug toxicity\nUse of nonsynergistic antimicrobial drug combination therapy\nSurgical drainage of the infected site\nDevelopment of antimicrobial drug resistance\nOne of the following statements is most appropriate regarding a hospital antibiotic formulary\nUsually determined by the director of pharmacy\nResults in increased resistance rates.\nIs switched every year depending on drug cost.\nEncourages familiarity with core antimicrobials.\nWhich of the following combinations would most likely contribute to the development of a superinfection?\nlong-term use of narrow-spectrum antimicrobials\nlong-term use of broad-spectrum antimicrobials\nshort-term use of narrow-spectrum antimicrobials\nshort-term use of broad-spectrum antimicrobials\n The consequences of antimicrobial resistance include all the following except?\nHigher risk for disability and death\nHigher healthcare cost\nLonger length of stay in hospital\nIncreased usage of narrow spectrum antibiotics\nWhich of the following is NOT effective in preventing the emergence or spread of antibiotic resistant pathogens\nAdherence to hand hygiene\nContact isolation during hospitalisation for patients colonised with MRSA\nTreating infections for a longer duration\nAvoiding the use of antibiotics for viral infections\nWhich is the MOST important action in combating antimicrobial resistance?\nImproving antibiotic use/ antibiotic stewardship\nDeveloping newer antibiotics and diagnostic tests\nImmunization\nHandwashing\nA 38 year old male was recently diagnosed with active tuberculosis while incarcerated He was started on standard four drug therapy (RHZE). Baseline lab values were ALT (SGPT) 42 units/L and AST (SGOT) 63 units/L. He was released from jail and his care was transferred to the local health department where an assessment revealed a social history of previous alcohol and cocaine use, bipolar disorder, and homelessness. What increases his risk of hepatotoxicity while taking TB medications?\nAlcohol use\nCocaine use\nBipolar disorder\nHomelessness\nA 38-year-old male was recently diagnosed with active tuberculosis while incarcerated He was started on standard four drug therapy (RHZE). Baseline lab values were ALT (SGPT) 42 units/L and AST (SGOT) 63 units/L. He was released from jail and his care was transferred to the local health department where an assessment revealed a social history of previous alcohol and cocaine use, bipolar disorder, and homelessness. During his follow up visit the patient complained that he was not feeling well. He admitted that he was still taking alcohol. His laboratory results revealed an increase in the ALT to 304 U/L and AST to 245 U/L. He was evaluated by the local health department physician, TB medications were stopped, he was instructed to return to the clinic for repeat labs, and he was re-educated to abstain from alcohol. What is the best approach to continue TB treatment?\nRestart all the TB medications once LFTs are normal.\nA drug rechallenge should be initiated once the ALT returns to \u003c 2x the upper limit of normal.\nDiscontinue TB treatment and have the patient follow-up every six months for symptoms of TB.\nRefer the patient to an alcohol rehabilitation center and restart all TB medications.\nA definitive diagnosis of tuberculosis is made with a:\nChest x-ray\nSputum smear\nSputum culture\nGenexpert\nA patient is admitted with pneumonia. Sputum cultures show that the patient is infected with a gram positive bacterium. The patient is allergic to Penicillin. Which medication would the patient most likely be prescribed?*\nMacrolide\nCephalosporins\nPencillin G\nAmoxicillin\nA person with Legionella pneumophila pneumonia can be best treated with:\nVancomycin.\nlinezolid.\nErythromycin.\nceftazidime.\nChoose the most common organism which causes lobar pneumonia in children:\nStaphylococcus aureus\nHaemophilus influenzae\nKlebsiella pneumoniae\nStreptococcus pneumonia\nMohan is a 53-year-old man who visits your office with a month of productive cough, 8 kilograms of weight loss, and night sweats. He has type II diabetes, for which he takes glyburide 5mg twice a day. He has a pleasant demeanor and you learn he is an avid cricket fan. In your initial investigation, you find that a chest X-ray shows a right upper lobe infiltrate, and sputum microscopy shows 2+ acid fast bacilli. He denies ever having had TB previously. You diagnose Mohan with pulmonary tuberculosis (TB) and decide to initiate him on once-daily treatment with isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol (RHZE). He weighs 53 kilograms at the time of your initial evaluation. As you start Mohan on TB therapy, which of the following IS NOT a counseling point you should provide to counter potential medication adverse effects and to minimize drug-drug interactions, so that he is more likely to adhere and complete therapy?\nAdvise him that he should not worry if his urine or tears turn red-orange color while on therapy\nTell him that his TB medications should only be taken at bedtime\nPrescribe pyridoxine (vitamin B6) 50mg by mouth daily\nAdvise him to visit his diabetes doctor soon after starting TB therapy, due to a risk of poorer blood sugar control while on TB therapy\nMycobacterium tuberculosis bacteria avoid innate immunity by\nInterference with phagosome-lysosome fusion\nPresence of a polysaccharide capsule\nDestruction of complement components\nLiving in an immuno-privileged tissue site\nOne of the following is a feature of community acquired pneumonia (CAP)\nContracted by a person with little contact with the healthcare system.\nUsually caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa.\nMost cases are caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae.\nThe most common cause is Haemophilus influenzae Type B (HIB)\n The difference between latent TB infection and active TB disease is that:\nPeople with latent TB infection are not infectious whereas people with TB disease are sometimes infectious\nOnly TB disease can be detected by a tuberculin skin test; latent TB infection cannot\nPeople with latent TB infection are infectious whereas people with TB disease are not\nLatent TB infection is curable but TB disease is not\nThe most common early symptoms of pulmonary TB are\nDry cough.\nRales and rhonchi.\nChest pain upon inspiration.\nProductive cough, malaise, and weight loss.\nThe traditional approach of anti-Pseudomonal drugs combination while treating HAP is:\nAntipseudomonal Beta lactam with a Fluoroquinolone\nAntipseudomonal Beta lactam with Aminoglycoside \nAntipseudomonal Beta lactam with a penicillin\nAntipseudomonal Beta lactam with a quinolone\nTypical bacterial pneumonia is caused by one of the following:\nMycoplasma pneumonia\nLegionnaires pneumonia\nPneumocystis carinii\nStaphylococcus aureus\nWhich of the following is true regarding CURB-65 score:\nRespiratory rate scores higher than hypotension\nHypoxaemia is a core adverse prognostic factor\nA Score of 3 to 5 mandates admission to the intensive care unit\nAntibiotics should be considered if the score is .2\nWhich of the following medical conditions places a person at the highest risk of developing TB disease after becoming infected:\nHIV infection\nDiabetes\nLess than ideal body weight\nPneumonia\nWhich of the following statements is true of hepatotoxicity induced by antiTB drugs?\nDrug-induced hepatitis is the most frequent serious adverse reaction of the first-line drugs.\nOther causes of abnormal liver tests must be excluded before diagnosing drug-induced hepatitis.\nIf LFTs are consistent with hepatotoxicity, all hepatotoxic drugs-must be stopped.\nAll the statements are true\nWhich of the following would be the most appropriate therapy for the treatment of Mycoplasma pneumonia in a patient with compliance issues and currently receiving theophylline?\nErythromycin\nAzithromycin\nClindamycin\nClarithromycin\nWhich of the following would you use for aspiration pneumonia with an intention to cover anaerobes?\nPiperacillin-Tazobactam\nCefepime\nAmpicillin-sulbactam\nCefazolin\nWithin the body, TB organisms:\nAre engulfed and killed by macrophages.\nReside indefinitely in an active stage in the body.\nAre walled off by macrophages in encapsulated granulomas called tubercles.\nReplicate and gain access to lymphatic channels through the bloodstream.\nAccording to the Jones Criteria, which one of the following sets of symptoms would indicate a positive diagnosis for Rheumatic fever?\nCarditis, fever, and an elevated WBC count\nPositive strep throat culture, arthritis, chorea\nElevated ESR, Carditis, fever, and arthralgia\nElevated C-reactive protein, Carditis, and fever\nAcute rheumatic fever (ARF) is\nsuppurative sequel of group A streptococcus infection\nHeart disease that occurs two to four days after group A streptococcus pharyngitis.\nHeart disease that occurs 2- 4 weeks after group A streptococcus pharyngitis.\nheart disease that occurs 2- 4 weeks following group B streptococcus pharyngitis\nPrevention of initial attack of rheumatic fever (primary prevention) is accomplished through all the following interventions EXCEPT?\nPrompt diagnosis\nAppropriate choice of antibiotic treatment of group A streptococcal tonsillopharyngitis\nAppropriate duration of antibiotic treatment for group A streptococcal tonsillopharyngitis\nUsing antibiotics to treat viral tonsillopharyngitis\nRegarding infective endocarditis which among the following is the most common bacteria in drug abusers?\nS. Epidermidis\nS. viridans\nS. Aureus\nS. pyogenes\nRheumatic.Fever (RF) usually follows an infection with:\nStreptococcus group A\nStaphylococcus aureus\nStreptococcus viridans\nH.influenza\nSub-Acute endocarditis usually follows an infection with:\nStreptococcus group A\nStaphylococcus aureus\nStreptococcus viridans\nH.influenza\nThe minor criteria of rheumatic fever includes?\nMigratory arthralgia\nCarditis and valvulitis\nErythema marginatum\nSubcutaneous nodules\nThe probability of acute rheumatic fever is high when there is___________\nGroup A streptococcal infection followed by two major manifestations or one major and two minor manifestations\nTwo major manifestations\nOne major and two minor manifestations.\nTwo minor manifestations\nTo diagnose a patient with R.F, The patient should have at least:\nOne major, two minor\nTwo major , one minor\nOne major , one minor\nTwo major, two minor\nWhich of the following antibiotic regimens would be the most appropriate for a patient with viridans group streptococcal IE (penicillin MIC \u003c 0.0125 mcg/mL [0.0125 mg/L]) in a patient reported allergy to penicillin (rash)?\nAmpicillin\nPenicillin G\nCefazolin\nCeftriaxone\nWhich of the following is considered major criteria in the diagnosis of IE?\nA pre-existing murmur which has now become significantly worse\nA patient with an endocardial abscess\nA positive blood culture with Klebsiella pneumoniae in two separate blood cultures drawn 12 hours apart\nA patient who has a history of IV drug abuse for the past 5 years\nWhich of the following is one of Jone’s major criteria?\nPancarditis\nFever\nElevated ESR\nPrevious RF\nWhich of the following is the best synergistic combination for treatment of a native-valve enterococcal endocarditis caused by strains susceptible to penicillin, aminoglycosides, and vancomycin?\nAmpicillin plus gentamicin\nAmpicillin plus tobramycin\nAmpicillin plus rifampin\nVancomycin plus rifampin\nWhich of the following is the most common manifestation of rheumatic fever\nCarditis\narthritis\nchorea\nSubcutaneous nodules\nWhich of the following patients should be recommended a prophylactic regimen prior to undergoing a major dental procedure involving manipulation of gingival tissue?\nA 4-year-old patient with repaired congenital heart defect at age 2\nA patient with a cardiac pacemaker\nA patient who recently received coronary artery stents after a heart attack\nA patient who had streptococcal endocarditis 20 years ago\nWhich of the following statements BEST characterizes arthritis in Rheumatic fever?\nA) Inflammation affecting single joint only\nB) Inflammation affects several joints; each joint inflammation lasts for few days to a week.\nC) Always associated with subcutaneous nodules\nD) Non-migratory.\nWhich of the following statements is FALSE regarding infective endocarditis (IE)?\nCoagulase-negative staphylococci are the predominant organism causing prosthetic valve endocarditis (PVE).\nPatients with HACEK organisms present acutely.\nIVDUs are at an increased risk of developing IE caused by less common organisms.\nA patient is more likely to present as a culture-negative if recently received antimicrobials.\nWhich of the following treatments would be the MOST appropriate empiric treatment regimen for a PVE (prosthetic valve endocarditis) with S. aureus?\nPenicillin G plus gentamicin plus rifampin for 6 weeks\nNafcillin plus gentamicin for 4 weeks plus surgery\nVancomycin plus gentamicin for 4 weeks plus rifampin for 2 weeks\nVancomycin plus rifampin for 6 weeks plus gentamicin for 2 weeks\nWhich of the following valves is mostly affected in drug abusers?\nAortic\nMitral\nTricuspid\nBicuspid\nWhich valves are commonly affected by R.F?\nTricuspid, mitral\nMitral, Aortic\nTricuspid, pulmonary\nAortic, pulmonary\nWhy is Prophylaxis against group A streptococcal (GAS) infection important?\nTo prevent recurrent rheumatic cardiac disease\nTo prevent recurrence of tonsillitis\nTo prevent joints deformity\nTo prevent arthritis\nA 30 year old pregnant woman develops tuberculosis. Which of the following antitubercular drugs should not be used:\nINH\nRifampicin\nStreptomycin\nEthambutol\n Which of the following are considered first-line drugs for TB?\nIsoniazid and clarithromycin\nRifampin and clofazimine\nEthambutol and isoniazid\nStreptomycin and cycloserine\n All the following are the symptoms of active TB EXCEPT?\nWeight loss\nNight sweats\nLoss of appetite\nHaematemesis\n An 82-year-old patient with documented pneumococcal pneumonia is unresponsive to 3 days of ceftriaxone. Gram-positive diplococci are present. Which action is most appropriate to take?\nIncrease the dose of ceftriaxone\nInitiate Vancomycin Therapy\nInitiate Cefazolin\nInitiate Ampicillin/Sulbactam\n A previously healthy 10-month-old patient was diagnosed for the first time with acute otitis media and was treated with amoxicillin 90 mg/kg daily. After 72 hours, the patient\u0027s fever and irritability persist. On examination, the right tympanic membrane is still bulging, red, and inflamed. Which treatment is most appropriate at this time?\nContinue amoxicillin 90mg/kg/day\nChange to amoxicillin/clavulanic acid (amoxicillin dosed at 90mg/kg/day)\nChange to Ceftriaxone 50mg/kg/day\nChange to levofloxacin\n Haemophilus influenzae is responsible for meningitis in which category of people?\nNeonates\nExtremely aged people\nInfants and children\nYoung Adults\nClassic symptoms of meningitis usually include;\nFever, Headache, Stiff neck\nBackache, Hallucination, Indigestion\nRash, Inner ear pain, Itching\nDry skin, Dehydration, Cold sores \n A 19-year old architectural student is admitted from university halls of residence with a 24-hour history of headache, nausea, vomiting and listlessness. There is neck stiffness on physical examination. Which physical sign would preclude the taking of a cerebrospinal fluid via lumbar puncture?\nA positive Kernig’s sign\nTachycardia, i.e. heart rate above 150 per minute, and hypotension\nPurpuric skin rash\nBlurred disc on ophthalmoscopy\nMeningitis caused by Listeria monocytogenes is most likely in which category of patient?\nCystic fibrosis\nHodgkin’s disease\nRecent travel abroad to the Tropics\nIngestion of bioactive yogurt in the preceding 24 hours\nRifampicin is administered as prophylaxis to close contacts of meningitis caused by which organism?\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\nListeria monocytogenes\nNeisseria meningitidis group C\nMycobacterium tuberculosis\nWhich of the following is used in the treatment of meningococcal meningitis in patients allergic to penicillin?\nCiprofloxacin\nTeicoplanin\nMeropenem\nChloramphenicol \nWhich of the following is false regarding the Gram stain test?\nIt is a complicated and time-consuming procedure.\nIn conjunction with microscopic examination, it provides presumptive diagnosis and some identification of the potential bacterial pathogen.\nIt is performed routinely on cerebral spinal fluid, urethral smears, and abscess, effusion, and sputum specimens.\nThe procedure is one of the first tests run on a specimen brought to the laboratory.\n ___________is the most common and most important bacteria responsible for pharyngitis?\nCorynebacterium diphtheriae\nHaemophilus influenzae (type B)\nStreptococcal tonsillitis\nStrep. Pyogenes (group A B-hemolytic)\nSECTION B: SHORT ANSWER ALL THE QUESTIONS (20 MARKS)\nOutline any four minor manifestations used in the diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever            \n                                                                                                                                      (4 Marks)\nIllustrate 4 symptoms and signs that should raise suspicion of pharyngitis of viral etiology             \n                                                                                                                                     (4 Marks)\nA 38-year old patient with a positive history of treatment for PTB 5 years ago presents to the chest clinic with chief complaints of malaise for the last 3 weeks, excessive sweating at night and unintended weight loss. Sputum for Gene Expert tests positive for rifampicin resistant MTB. Identify the diagnosis and design a five-drug treatment plan for this patient  (6 Marks)\nDescribe any six core elements of establishing an antimicrobial stewardship program                 \n                                    (6 Marks)\nSECTION C: LONG ESSAY QUESTIONS (ANSWER ANY ONE QUESTION ONLY) (20 MARKS)\nA 19-year-old college student is brought to the emergency department with confusion. According to the roommate the patient began vomiting and complaining of a headache about 18-24 hours ago. She has no medical problems or sick contacts. She has no history of smoking but drinks alcohol moderately on weekends. Her temperature is 390C, her pulse is 117/min, RR is 14/min and blood pressure is 94/56 mmHg. She is lethargic and is oriented to self but not to time or location. Her pupils are bilaterally equal in size and reactive to light. Nuchal rigidity is present. Her extremities are somewhat cool to touch and her wrists and lower legs show petechiae. Physical examination reveals no other abnormality. Within 24 hours of admission into the critical care unit, the petechial rash on her extremities has developed into widespread purple lesions on her extremities, trunk and face.\nIdentify the most probable diagnosis and the likely causative agent             (2 Marks)\nJustify your answer in (a) above.                                                                    (3 Marks)\nDescribe (3) THREE investigations that you would recommend in order to support your diagnosis                                                                                                 (3 Marks)\nReview (5) FIVE  patient related factors that you would consider while selecting empirical antimicrobial therapy for this patient                                              (5 Marks)\nPropose an appropriate treatment plan for this patient                                   (4 Marks)\nList TWO (2) complications that you would be worried about in this patient(1 Mark)\nExplain how you would deal with the contacts of this patient                       (2 Marks)\nMr. JW, age 40 years, lives at home and was previously healthy, but saw his physician one day ago, 5 days after returning from a conference abroad, where he had stayed in a large hotel and indulged his passion for frequent whirlpool baths. He Presented with dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain and sudden development of fever and cough, producing rusty sputum. He was disorientated. His respiratory rate was 33/min and SpO2 was 85%. His temperature was 38.80C. His urea was 8.5mmol/L. His BP was 100/68 mmHg and PR of 110B/min.\nIdentify the most likely diagnosis including the most likely causative organism            \n \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t          (2 Marks)\nJustify your answer in (a) above                                      (6 points @0.5 marks each)\noutline (2) TWO additional investigations that you would recommend      (2 Marks)\nEvaluate whether you would recommend treating this patient as an outpatient, inpatient (general ward) or in an intensive care unit. Justify your answer.   (4 Marks)\nFormulate a treatment plan for this patient                                                  (5 Marks)\nUsing appropriate examples examine how you would monitor for response to treatment                                                                                                       (4 Marks)","exam-respiratory-renal":"KABARAK                         UNIVERSITY\nUNIVERSITY EXAMINATIONS\nMAIN CAMPUS\nTHIRD SEMESTER, 2018/2019 ACADEMIC YEAR\nEXAMINATION FOR BACHELOR DEGREE IN PHARMACY\n     UNIT CODE: PHAM 3316\tUNIT TITLE: CLINICAL PHARMACY II (RESPIRATORY AND RENAL)\nSTREAM: Y3S3\t\t\t\t\t\t  TIME: 2 HOURS\nEXAMINATION SESSION: JULY 2019\t\t\t   DATE: JULY, 2019\n        _______________________________________________________________________\nREAD INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY\nThe examination paper has THREE (3) sections.       [Total 100 Marks]\nAnswer ALL questions in sections A, B and TWO in C\nSECTION A has TWENTY Multiple Choice Questions.         [20 Marks]\nSECTION B has EIGHT Short Answer Questions.               [40 Marks]\nSECTION C has THREE Long Answer Questions.               [40 Marks]\nAnswer questions in the BOOKLET provided. Begin each of the questions in section C in a new page of the answer booklet. \nAdhere to the University examination rules and regulations\nDo not write anything on the question paper.\nSECTION A: MULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS (20 MARKS). \nAnswer all questions. Choose the best answer for each question.\nThe following are possible complications of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease:\nPermanent changes in the function of the lungs due to remodeling of the airways\nCo-pulmonale\nRespiratory failure\nAll the above\nBoth a and b\nThe following is correct regarding the FEV1 EXCEPT:\nIs the fraction of the vital capacity that is expelled during the first minute of a forced expiration\nUsually reduced in asthma due to the bronchoconstriction that causes airway resistance\nVaries depending on age, sex, height, mass and race\nGiven in litres/min or as a percentage of the average value of healthy individuals of similar age, sex, height, mass and race\nValues between 80 – 120% of average are considered normal\nThe following agents can be used in the management of patients who wish to quit smoking EXCEPT\nNicotine containing chewing gum, lozenges or dermal patches\nTheophylline gum\nVarenicicline\nBupropion\nAll the above\nThe following classes of drugs are used in the management of COPD\nN-acetyl cysteine\nMast cell stabilisers\nAlpha-1 antitrypsin\nBoth (a) and (c)\nAll the above\nRegarding the use of beta agonists in asthma, which statement is FALSE\nInhaled short acting agents are the most effective agents for reversing acute airway obstruction caused by bronchoconstriction \nOral short acting agents are effective in acute asthma \nFormoterol can be used for acute relief of symptoms\nLong acting beta agonists are indicated for add-on therapy for asthma not controlled on low to medium doses of inhaled corticosteroids\nChoose the FALSE statement:\nAddition of a long-acting inhaled β2-agonist to inhaled corticosteroid therapy reduces the amount of inhaled corticosteroids necessary for asthma control\nCombined therapy with a long-acting inhaled β2-agonist and an inhaled corticosteroid is superior to the combination of a leukotriene modifier or theophylline with an inhaled corticosteroid\nFormoterol \u0026 salmeterol can be used as monotherapy for chronic asthma\nSalbutamol is more effective than nedocromil sodium for prophylaxis of exercise-induced asthma\nMast cell stabilisers are only effective as prophylactic agents and can also be used in other allergic conditions\nThe following are synergistic combinations EXCEPT:\nTheophylline and inhaled corticosteroid \nShort acting beta agonist and short acting anticholinergic agent\nInhaled corticosteroid and antimuscarinic agent\nLong acting beta agonist and inhaled corticosteroid\nAll the above\nThe following statements are correct regarding the use of corticosteroids in asthma EXCEPT:\nThey are the most comprehensive control of the inflammatory process and are the cornerstone of therapy in persistent asthma \nThey are effective as both long-term control \u0026 rescue medications\nTheir onset of effects is delayed \u0026 a clinical response may not be seen for 4-12 hours\nIt is not necessary to taper the systemic steroid dose in patients receiving short bursts of systemic corticosteroid therapy\nThey are more effective when administered as monotherapy \nThe following are risk factors for chronic renal failure EXCEPT:\nHypertension\nDiabetes\nObesity\nFamily history of kidney disease\nTobacco smoking\nThe following are side effects of ipratropium EXCEPT:\nUrinary retention \ndryness of the mouth\noral candidiasis\nunpleasant bitter taste\nglaucoma\nThe pathophysiology of COPD involves all the following EXCEPT:\nOxidative stress\nHyper-responsiveness of airways\nDestruction of pulmonary vasculature\nDestruction of lung parenchyma\nChronic inflammation\nTheophylline is no longer considered a first line therapy in the management of asthma or COPD due to the following reasons EXCEPT:\nVariations in plasma drug concentrations among patients hence the need for therapeutic drug monitoring \nPotential for drug interactions with many other drugs\nPotential to cause liver damage\nAbility to precipitate cardiac arrhythmias\nAbility to cause seizures\nThe following are classical signs and symptoms of  asthma EXCEPT:\nShortness of breath\nTachypnoea\nHyper-resonance on percussion\nCough\nWheezing\nThe pathophysiology of asthma involves all the following EXCEPT:\nOxidative stress\nHyper-responsiveness of airways\nAirway remodelling\nDestruction of lung parenchyma\nChronic inflammation\nThe following inflammatory cells are involved in the pathophysiology of COPD EXCEPT:\nNeutrophils\nT-Lymphocytes\nMast cells\nMacrophages\nEosinophils\nThe following are mediators involved in the pathophysiology of asthma EXCEPT:\nProstaglandins\nLeukotrienes\nHistamine\nBradykinin\nAll the above are mediators\nThe following is considered a chronic obstructive pulmonary disease EXCEPT:\nNocturnal hypoxia\nAcute bronchitis\nBronchiectasis\nObstructive sleep apnoea\nCystic Fibrosis\nThe following are findings on the chest x-ray of patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease EXCEPT:\nsevere inflation of the lung\nlow flattened diaphragm\nLong and narrow heart\nTracheal shift \nAll the above are present\nBronchiectasis is:\nA disease characterized by permanent enlargement of the airspaces distal to the terminal bronchioles, accompanied by destruction of their walls without obvious fibrosis\na disease characterized by cough, production of large amounts of sputum and dilated and thickened bronchi with frequent bacterial infections\nAlteration in the viscosity and tenacity of mucus produced at epithelial surfaces\nChronic productive cough for at least 3 months in each of two consecutive years in a patient in whom other causes have been excluded\nNone of the above is correct\nThe following is a risk factor for asthma:\nObesity\nEnvironment\nParacetamol\nRespiratory infections\nExtreme emotions\nSECTION B: ANSWER ALL QUESTIONS (40 MARKS)\nBriefly discuss the investigations that are involved in the diagnosis of asthma \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(5 Marks)\nOutline the TEN steps involved in the accurate use of a metered dose inhaler \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(5 Marks)\nWhat are the complications of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(5marks)\nWhat monitoring parameters are required in the effective follow up of patients with asthma?  \t\t\t\t\t\t(5Marks)\nBriefly discuss the management of a common cold \t(5 marks)\nBriefly discuss the clinical presentation of chronic renal failure\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(5marks)\nBriefly discuss the management of acute respiratory distress syndrome \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(5marks)\nBriefly discuss the management of acute renal failure\t(5 Marks)\nSECTION C: ANSWER ANY TWO QUESTIONS (40 MARKS)\n1. Mr. Barasa presents to you with symptoms of cough, wheezing, dyspnea and chest tightness for one month. He reports that the symptoms occur daily and interfere with his ability to go to work or even carry out normal routine activities at home. He is a road works contractor and is currently involved in the construction of a new highway in his county. He reports night time symptoms twice every week and has to wake up to use his inhaler. Besides the inhaler which he was told by is doctor to use whenever he experiences the symptoms of wheezing and dyspnea, he also reports using salbutamol tablets and prednisolone tablets. He however reports that he does not use them consistently because of the tremors and palpitations that he experiences. He has come to you because last night, the inhaler was not able to relieve the symptoms and he had a troublesome night.\nWhat condition is Mr. Barasa suffering from? \t\t(2 Marks)\nHow would you classify the condition and why? \t\t(2 Marks)\nGiving examples, describe the classes of medication that Mr. Barasa would benefit from? \t\t\t\t\t\t(8 Marks)\nWhat interventions would you make with regards to his current medication? \t\t\t\t\t\t\t(3 Marks)\nWhat side effects would you counsel the patient on before starting them on your choice of medication \t\t\t\t(5 Marks)\n2. Catherine presents to you with complaints of cough, severe dyspnoea and production of copious amounts of sputum for the past 6 months. On examination, she presents with cyanosis, tachypnea, digital clubbing and use of accessory muscles to breathe. On further history taking you realise that Catherine is a cook by profession and sells githeri and uji to construction workers around her home\nWhat is Catherine likely to be suffering from? \t(2 Marks)\nWhat are some of the investigations that you would request for Catherine? \t\t\t\t\t\t\t(5 Marks)\nWhat classes of medication would you recommend for Catherine?\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(8Marks)\nWhat non-pharmacological interventions would you recommend for the patient? \t\t\t\t\t\t\t(5marks)\n3. While attending ward rounds, you meet J.B. who suffers from a pulmonary condition that presents with a cough, dyspnea, purse lip breathing and speaks in short jerky sentences. He reports that the symptoms began 5 years ago and has been progressing despite regular use of his medication. You notice that he is on oxygen therapy and on inquiry he reports that he is comfortable only when on oxygen.\nWhat could JB be suffering from? (Support your answer) (2 Marks)\nWhat risk factors are associated with this condition? \t(5marks)\nWhat medication should JB be using that would help to manage the disease and slow down the disease progression? \t\t(8 Marks)\nWhat parameters would you use as part of pharmaceutical care to monitor this patient in subsequent visits? \t\t\t(5 Marks)","exam-infections-ii":"KABARAK                         UNIVERSITY\nUNIVERSITY EXAMINATIONS\nMAIN CAMPUS\nFIRST SEMESTER, 2020/2021 ACADEMIC YEAR\nEXAMINATION FOR BACHELOR DEGREE IN PHARMACY\nPHAM 4220: CLINICAL PHARMACY VI (INFECTIONS II)\nSTREAM: Y4S2\t\t\t\t\t\t                TIME: 2:00-5:00PM\nEXAMINATION SESSION: SEPT-DEC \t   \t\t       DATE: 05/02/2021\nREAD INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY\nThe examination paper has THREE (3) sections.                                  [Total 100 Marks]\nAnswer ALL questions in sections A, B and ONLY ONE IN C\nSECTION A has sixty Multiple Choice Questions.                                            [60 Marks]\nSECTION B has FIVE Short Answer Questions.                                               [20 Marks]\nSECTION C has TWO Long Answer Questions.                                               [20 Marks]\nAnswer questions in the BOOKLET provided. Begin each of the questions in a new page of the answer booklet. \nAdhere to the University examination rules and regulations\nDo not write anything on the question paper.\nSECTION A: ANSWER ALL THE QUESTIONS                                            (60 MARKS)\nWhat is the main clinical picture of hepatitis A?\nRash\nLimb paralysis\nJaundice and abdominal discomfort\nUpper respiratory tract infection\nThe following are associated with an increased risk of vertical transmission of HIV except?\nbreast feeding\nhigh titres of p24 antigen in maternal serum perinatally\nlow maternal CD4 counts during pregnancy\nprolonged labour\nContamination of soil with excreta of cats transmit:\nToxoplasma \nVisceral larva migrans\nCutaneous larva migrans \nAll of the above.\nAn 89-year-old man presents to casualty with a fever and a several week history of headache. On examination he is confused, has neck stiffness and a right seventh cranial nerve palsy. He has no visible rash. A lumbar puncture reveals CSF Protein 4.0 g/l, Glucose 1.2 mmol/l (plasma glucose 4.6 mmol/l), and on microscopy 300 white cells/µl, predominantly lymphocytes. Serum VDRL is positive and TPHA is negative. The most likely diagnosis is:\nviral meningitis\nneurosyphilis\nherpes simplex encephalitis\ntuberculous meningitis\nListeria meningitis \n An 18-year-old male has been seen in the clinic for urethral discharge.\nHe is treated with ceftriaxone, but the discharge has not resolved and the culture has returned as no growth. Which of the following is the most likely etiologic agent to cause this infection?\nCeftriaxone-resistant gonococci\nChlamydia psittaci\nChlamydia trachomatis\nHerpes simplex\n A 48-year-old Senegalese man was found to be HIV positive on routine screening with a fourth-generation combined p24 Ag/Ab ELISA. A confirmatory test with a second-generation ELISA for IgG to differentiate between HIV-1 and HIV-2 infection detected HIV-2 antibodies. He was negative for HIV-1 antibodies and an HIV-1 RNA viral load was negative. An HIV-2 RNA viral load was sent to the reference laboratory. His CD4 count was 458 cells/µL (mm3). What class of antiretrovirals is HIV-2 intrinsically resistant to?\nChemokine receptor 5 (CCR5) antagonists\nIntegrase inhibitors\nNon-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors\nNucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors\nThe commonest bacterial cause of urinary tract infection is:\nEsherichia coli\nEnterococcus faecalis\nStaphylococcus saprophyticus\nProteus mirabilis\nGlobally, the leading viral cause of hepato-cellular carcinoma is:\nHAV\nHBV\nHCV\nHDV \n Children should be given an HAV vaccine when they are:\nIn their second year of life\nAt 2-5 years\nAt 5-10 years\nIn their first year of life\nIndividuals who test negative for anti-HCV antibodies:\nAre not infected with HCV\nShould be confirmed negative using the recombinant immunoblot assay\nShould be confirmed negative using a nucleic acid test\nShould be treated for subclinical HCV infection\nAn individual who is positive only for anti-HBc-IgG + anti-HBs is:\nAcutely infected\nChronically infected\nImmune after recovering from infection\nNot infected Immune because they have been effectively vaccinated\n Drug resistance is a major problem for which of the following STIs?\nChlamydia\nTreponema pallidum\nN. gonorrhoeae\nShigella\nA skin rash on the hands and feet is typical of which of the following STIs?\nChlamydia\nTreponema pallidum\nN. gonorrhoeae\nHepatitis C \nWhich of the following virus is NOT transmitted through the parenteral route?\nHepatitis A\nHepatitis B\nHepatitis C\nHepatitis D \nHepatitis E virus (HEV) infection is more commonly found in developing countries which is transmitted mostly through contaminated water, all of the following are the signs and symptoms of HEV, EXCEPT?\nBlood in the stool\nNausea and vomiting\nYellowing of the skin\nLow-grade fever \n All of the following statements are true for the diagnosis of HBV infection, EXCEPT?\nTests for the detection of elevated liver enzymes should be done\nSerological tests to detect the antigen\nLiver function test- bilirubin\nGlucose challenge test     \n. What route transmits hepatitis A and E?\nTactile\nDroplet\nFecal/oral\nBloodborne \nSigns and symptoms of acute hepatitis A infection include all of the following, EXCEPT:\nPedal edema\nHepatomegaly\nFever and chills\nNausea and vomiting \nHepatitis E is typically a self-limiting disease, except in\nIV drug users.\nPregnant women.\nHIV-positive patients.\nPatients with pre-existing obstructive liver disease. \nA patient\u0027s hepatitis B antibody panel is positive for both HBcIgG and HBsAb, but negative for HBsAg. The correct interpretation of these results is that the patient\nIs chronically infected with hepatitis B.\nHas never been exposed to hepatitis B virus.\nHas had acute hepatitis B in the past, but the infection has now resolved.\nHad a chronic case of hepatitis B in the past, but the disease responded to interferon therapy. \nHepatitis B vaccine is typically administered as a series of\nThree injections.\nFour injections.\nFive injections.\nSix injections. \nWhich of the following statements is TRUE regarding direct-acting antiviral drug combinations for the treatment of chronic HCV infection?\nThey do not require parenteral administration (can be taken orally).\nThe achievable sustained viral remission (SVR) rate is more than 90%.\nThey produce better results, in less time, with fewer side effects than interferon alfa.\nAll of the above \nWhich is NOT a common cause of UTIs in men?\nErectile dysfunction\nCatheter usage\nUrinary stone\nEnlarged prostate \nAcute hemorrhagic cystitis is most commonly caused by:\nE.coli\nAdenovirus\nKlebsiella\nProteus \nThe drug of choice for UTI Prophylaxis in a 2-month old infant is:\nCefixime\nCefadroxil\nCotrimoxazole\nCephalexin \nA patient you are treating in the clinic asks you what actions they can take to help prevent urinary tract infections. All of the following statements are correct, EXCEPT? \nWipe one’s genital area front to back after using the restroom.\nWear tight-fitting clothing.\nEmpty one’s bladder after sexual intercourse.\nDrink lots of water.\nWhich of the following scenarios of patients is least likely to acquire a UTI?\nA 24-year-old male with poor hygiene.\nAn immunocompromised individual receiving chemotherapy.\nA post-menopausal woman with a history of diabetes mellitus.\nA 52-year-old female who had a urinary catheter placed for surgery.\nWhich of the following is not a common cause of UTI among men?\nUsing a catheter\nKidney stone\nErectile dysfunction\nProstate enlargement\n All the following are the signs of UTI EXCEPT?\nIncreased frequency of urination\nPainful urination\nMilky/cloudy urine\nPolyuria\nA 40 years old woman came with dysuria, fever, urine analysis showed neutrophils cast, what is the most likely diagnosis?\nAcute pyelonephritis\nChronic pyelonephritis\nAcute cystitis\nAcute prostatitis \nWhich of the following is not a recommended regimen for treatment of UTI in the non-pregnant woman?\ntrimethoprim 300 mg orally daily for 5 days\nAmoxicillin/clavulanic 1g BD for 5 days\ncephalexin 500 mg orally BD for 5 days\nnitrofurantoin 50 mg QID for 5 days\nWhich of the following drug therapies is inappropriate for treatment of UTI in pregnant women?\nAmoxicillin/clavulanic 1g BD\nnitrofurantoin 50mg QID\ncephalexin 250mg QID\ntrimethoprim 300mg daily  \nWhich of the following patients with pyelonephritis can be safely treated as an outpatient?\nA 75-year-old diabetic female\nA 20-year-old 20-week pregnant female with mild abdominal cramping\nA 30-year-old female with persistent vomiting and fever\nNone of the above can be safely treated as outpatients  \nA 22-year-old previously healthy male complains of dysuria. Genital examination is normal. Urinalysis shows 5 to 10 white blood cells per high power field. Which of the following antibiotic regimens is MOST appropriate?\nCiprofloxacin, 500 mg orally twice a day for 2 weeks\nTrimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, two tablets orally twice a day for 3 days\nAzithromycin, 1 g orally once, and Ofloxacin, 400 mg orally once\nCephalexin, 500 mg orally for 7 days\nAll of the following are risk factors for UTI EXCEPT?\nsexual intercourse\nuterine prolapse\nuse of diaphragm and spermacide\nirregular menses\nGenital herpes is most commonly caused by\nHerpes simplex virus 1.\nVaricella-zoster virus.\nHerpes simplex virus 2.\nCytomegalovirus. \nWhich oral medication is recommended as an initial topical treatment for genital yeast infections?\nfluconazole\nNystatin\nacyclovir\nmiconazole\nWhat is the only common infection of the reproductive tract caused by a protozoan?\ngonorrhea\nchlamydia\ntrichomoniasis\ncandidiasis\nAxel Tulip has a thin, clear discharge coming from his penis and mild discomfort when he urinates. He is most likely suffering from _______ and should be treated with ________.\ngonorrhea, penicillin\ngonorrhea, tetracycline\nchlamydia, penicillin\nchlamydia, doxycycline \nThe term \"seropositive\" is given to a person:\nwhen he or she develops full-blown AIDS\nwhen he or she tests positive for HIV\nat the time the first opportunistic infection is diagnosed\nwhen the T4 helper cell count drops below 500\nHerpes can be cured through topical application or ingestion of:\nacyclovir\npodophyllin\nceftriaxone\nFoscarnet\nLymphadenopathy is another term for which stage of AIDS?\nthe final stage\nasymptomatic carrier stage\nthe third stage of the syndrome\ninitial infection \n The term \"chancre\" is given to:\nthe sores symptomatic of herpes\nthe sore symptomatic of syphilis\nthe growths symptomatic of genital warts\nGonorrhea\nWhich of the following is not a risk factor for mother-to-child transmission of HIV?\nA high maternal viral load\nAdvanced maternal age\nA low maternal CD4 count\nProlonged rupture of membranes \nIn people infected with HIV in Africa, what is the most frequent cause of death?\nTuberculosis\nPneumocystis pneumonia\nHerpes simplex virus\nToxoplasma gondii encephalitis\nWhich of the following is NOT an adherence strategy for adults?\nEnlisting a treatment supporter\nDisclosing to a partner and family\nIndividual or group counseling if needed\nPeer support groups\nAdherence involves all of the following EXCEPT….?\nDisclosing HIV status to all family members.\nStoring the medication correctly.\nTaking the correct dose.\nRegularly attending clinic visits.\nWhich of the following is a direct method of measuring adherence?\nPill counts\nPharmacy refill rates\nDirectly observed therapy\nClinical response to therapy\nWhich of the following is a correct order for mitochondrial toxicity?\nDDI\u003eD4T\u003eAZT\nDDI\u003eAZT\u003eD4T\nD4T\u003eDDI\u003eAZT\nD4T\u003eAZT\u003eDDI\nWhich of the following is NOT associated with mitochondrial toxicity?\nPeripheral Neuropathy\nLactic Acidosis\nLypodystrophy\nSteven’s Johnson Syndrome\nA small proportion of individuals can resist infection by HIV because they lack the:\nCCR 5 receptor\nCXCR 4 receptor\nThe fusion domain\nThe GP41 receptor\nA 33-year-old man with HIV who has been out of medical care presents with a 2-week history of cough, malaise, and dyspnea with exertion. He is not taking any medications. Oxygen saturation is 82% at rest but decreases to 68% with ambulation. A diagnosis of of Pneumocystis pneumonia is strongly suspected (and subsequently confirmed). He has no known medication allergies. Which one of the following would you recommend to treat this man with Pneumocystis pneumonia?\nOral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole alone\nOral atovaquone\nOral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole plus prednisolone\nIntravenous pentamidine\n Which one of the following is an acceptable alternative to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for the treatment of this woman with moderate-to-severe Pneumocystis pneumonia?\nOral primaquine plus IV clindamycin\nInhaled pentamidine\nOral clindamycin\nOral dapsone plus oral primaquine\nWhich one of the following is recommended as first line therapy for a patient suspected to have Toxoplasma encephalitis?\nPyrimethamine plus leucovorin plus clindamycin\nAtovaquone plus azithromycin\nTrimethoprim plus dapsone plus micafungin\nPyrimethamine plus leucovorin plus sulfadiazine \n A 39-year-old man with no known medical problems is brought to the hospital with a 2-week history of headache, photophobia, nausea, vomiting, poor appetite, and night sweats. Computed tomographic (CT) brain scan showed no mass lesions and no midline shift. Lumbar puncture demonstrates an opening pressure of 17 cm H2O, and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) examination shows 16 cells/mm3 (85% lymphocytes), a glucose of 26 mg/dL, and a protein of 90.5 mg/dL. The CSF cryptococcal antigen is positive (titer of 1:2048). The serum cryptococcal antigen is positive (titer of 1:512). An HIV-1/2 antigen-antibody test is performed and is positive. Which one of the following would be recommended as the preferred ideal initial antifungal therapy for this man?\nCaspofungin plus fluconazole\nHigh dose Fluconazole\nAmphotericin B only\nLiposomal amphotericin B plus flucytosine\nA 29-year-old man with HIV is diagnosed with cryptococcal meningitis presented to the emergency room with fever, headache, confusion, and photophobia. A lumbar puncture revealed an opening pressure of 32 cm H2O and analysis of cerebrospinal fluid demonstrated positive cryptococcal antigen. In addition to starting appropriate antifungal therapy, what is the optimal management of the increased intracranial pressure?\nAdminister mannitol 2.0 g/kg IV and repeat in 24 hours if needed\nAdminister acetazolamide 500 mg IV and repeat in 12 hours if needed\nAdminister methylprednisolone 4 mg PO every 6 hours for 3 days\nPerform daily lumbar punctures with removal of CSF\nA 32-year-old woman with HIV and a CD4 count of 60 cells/mm3 was recently started on antiretroviral therapy, but after 3 weeks of treatment she presents to the clinic with a complaint of altered taste sensation and odynophagia. On physical examination she has thick white plaques on her tongue and oropharynx that scrape off when light pressure is applied with a tongue depressor. You make a clinical diagnosis of oral candidiasis and suspect she also has esophageal candidiasis. Which one of the following is recommended as the initial management for this problem?\nTherapy is not recommended unless biopsy confirms the diagnosis\nStart micafungin by intravenous infusion and if she responds continue for a total of 10 to 14 days\nStart nystatin suspension swish and swallow and if she responds continue for a total of 21 to 28 days\nStart oral fluconazole and if she responds continue for a total of 14 to 21 days \n A 25-year-old man presents to an urgent care clinic with a 3-week history of lower abdominal pain and cramping associated with watery, non-bloody diarrhea. He was recently diagnosed with HIV and has a CD4 count of 42 cells/mm3. A diagnosis of cryptosporidiosis is made. Which one of the following choices would likely have the biggest impact on improving the symptoms related to this gastrointestinal infection? \nStart antiretroviral therapy\nAzithromycin 500 mg once daily for 90 days, then start antiretroviral therapy\nParomomycin 500 mg three times daily for 90 days, then start antiretroviral therapy\nNitazoxanide 500 mg twice daily for 90 days, then start antiretroviral therapy\nIn addition to continuing antiretroviral therapy, which one of the following antimicrobial regimens is recommended as appropriate acute therapy for Isosporiasis?\nMetronidazole 500 mg three times daily for 14 days\nTrimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole 160/800 mg twice daily for 10 days\nAlbendazole 400 mg twice daily for 10 days\nCiprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for 3 days\nWhich among the following is not a symptom of pelvic inflammatory disease?\nLower Abdominal pain\nFoul-smelling vaginal discharge\nPainful sexual intercourse\nOliguria \nSECTION B: SHORT ANSWER ALL THE QUESTIONS                       (20 MARKS)\nCo-trimoxazole preventive therapy is a cost-effective strategy for preventing opportunistic infections in HIV positive patients. Identify four such infections that are usually prevented by CPT                                                                              (2 Marks)\nA 56-year old truck driver is admitted in hospital with a diagnosis of Cryptococcal meningitis. He is put on Amphotericin B among other medications. You are asked by the MO intern for advice regarding administration and monitoring of Amphotericin B. Design a summary of the recommendations you would make to the MO intern.                         \n                                                                                                                             (4 Marks)\nOutline three indirect methods that you would use to assess adherence to ART in HIV positive patients during routine comprehensive care clinic visits                   (3 Marks)\nExplain the current recommendations for prevention of recurrent HBV infection after liver transplantation                                                                        (2 Marks)\nb) Describe 3 goals of therapy for treating HCV infection.                           (3 Marks)\nH.C., a 19-year-old, sexually active woman, complains of mild dysuria, a purulent vaginal discharge, fever, and moderately severe, bilateral, lower abdominal pain of 3 days’ duration. Examination confirms uterine and adnexal tenderness, a purulent cervical exudate, and a temperature of 39â—¦C. Laboratory examinations show a nonreactive VDRL and negative urinalysis. A pregnancy test performed was negative. The peripheral WBC count was mildly elevated (11,000/μL) with 70% polymorphonuclear leukocytes. \nJustify a presumptive diagnosis of PID in this patient                                             \n                                                                                        (4 points @ 0.5 marks each)\nDevelop an appropriate treatment plan for this patient (3 antibiotic combination and any other adjunctive therapy)                                                            (4 Marks)\nSECTION C: LONG ESSAY QUESTIONS                                                          (20 MARKS)\nA 31-year-old man newly diagnosed with HIV was admitted with shortness of breath, cough and generalized chest discomfort. Chest radiograph revealed diffuse bilateral opacities and a blood gas analysis demonstrated an arterial partial oxygen pressure (PaO2) of 8.0 kPa (normal range 11.0–14.0 kPa). Sputum culture was uninformative. A Broncho alveolar lavage was performed and transbronchial biopsies taken.\nIdentify the most likely clinical diagnosis and the causative agent? (2 Marks)\nJustify your answer in (a) above                                  (4 points @0.5 marks each)\nPropose 2 other tests that could be performed to support the diagnosis (2 Marks)\nDesign a comprehensive treatment plan for this patient including appropriate antimicrobial regimen, adjunctive treatment and supportive therapy. (5 Marks)\nAssuming this patient was allergic to the first line treatment for this condition propose two other alternative pharmacological interventions (for moderate to severe infection) that you would consider for this infection                 (2 Marks)\nExplain how you would monitor treatment in this patient                   (2 Marks)\nIllustrate the ART regimen that you would recommend to be initiated in this patient and the exact timing of initiation of the regimen (5 Marks) (Justify your answer) \nD.S., a 23-year-old male army officer recently stationed in Lanet, complains of dysuria, meatal pain, and a profuse yellow urethral discharge for 2 days. He admits to extramarital sex with a sex worker during the past week. He is accompanied by his pregnant wife, C.S., who is asymptomatic. D.S. engages in vaginal sex, but there is no history of oral or anal sex with either partner. Intracellular gram-negative diplococci are seen on the Gram stain of D.S.’s urethral exudate.\nIdentify the most likely diagnosis and causative agent                             (2 Marks)\nExplain your answer in (a) above                               (4 points @ 0.5 marks each)\nPrepare the treatment plans for both C.S and D.S                                   (4 Marks)\nOutline three parameters that you would use to monitor response to treatment for both CS and D.S                                                                                     (3 Marks)\nIdentify one antimicrobial drug that is effective for this condition but is contraindicated in C.S because of pregnancy (1 Mark). Give the reason for contraindication                                                                                        (1 mark)\nAssuming D.S presented before 72 hours elapsed after initial sexual contact with the sex worker, outline any other therapy that he would have benefited from (Give the drug regimen and duration of therapy)                                              (3 Marks)\nList (2)TWO adverse effects associated with the therapy in (f) that you would watch out for                                                                                             (2 Marks)\nD.S is diagnosed with primary syphilis 3 years later. Outline (2) TWO possible complications of syphilis in D.S                                                              (2 Marks)","exam-cns":"    KABARAK                          UNIVERSITY\n         UNIVERSITY EXAMINATIONS\nMAIN CAMPUS\nFIRST SEMESTER, 2020/2021 ACADEMIC YEAR\nEXAMINATION FOR THE DEGREE OF BACHELOR IN PHARMACY\nPHAM 5121: CLINICAL PHARMACY VII (CENTRAL NERVOUS SYSTEM DISORDERS)\nSPECIAL EXAMS\nSTREAM: Y1S1\t\t\t\t\t\t           TIME: 9:00-11:00AM\nEXAMINATION SESSION: SEPT-DEC  \t\t\t DATE: 25/01/2021 \nREAD INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY\nThe examination paper has THREE (3) sections.                      [Total 100 Marks]\nAnswer ALL questions in sections A, B and  C\nSECTION A has SIXTY Multiple Choice Questions.                             [60 Marks]\nSECTION B has FIVE Short Answer Questions.                                  [20 Marks]\nSECTION C has TWO Long Answer Questions.                                    [20 Marks]\nAdhere to the University online examination rules and regulations\nSECTION A : ANSWER ALL QUESTIONS                                                                 (60 MARKS)\nYour typically quiet roommate has suddenly become annoyingly talkative. He kept you awake until 3:00 AM because he suddenly decided to build a bookcase, though he had never built anything before. Your roommate may be\nPsychotic.\nManic.\nDissociating.\nSchizotypal.\nWhich of the following is known to cause disulfiram-ethanol like reaction?\nPropranolol www. currentnursing.com\nHaloperidol\nCiprofloxacin\nMetronidazole\nWhich of the following is the most dependence-producing derivative of opium?\nMeperidine\nDiphenoxylate\nMethadone\nHeroin\nWhich of the following symptoms are NOT found in people with a schizophrenic disorder?\nIncoherent thinking and bizarre behaviour\nDelusions and hallucinations\nDisturbed affect\nHypersomnia and Scary dreams\nEthosuximide is the drug of choice for:\nGrand mal epilepsy\nPetit mal seizure\nMyoclonic seizure\nPartial Seizures\nWhich of the following is considered the key pathophysiologic event triggering secondary neuronal injury following an acute head injury?\nSystemic hypotension\nCentral nervous system acidosis\nIncreased cerebral blood flow\nCentral nervous system ischemia\nWhich of the following is an accepted first-line therapy in the management of severe head injury?\nAggressive hyperventilation (PaCO2 25-30 mm Hg)\nModerate hypothermia (34-35°C)\nMethylprednisolone\nMannitol\nWhich of the following Glasgow Coma Scale scores in a patient would be most consistent with severe head injury?\n4\n10\n14\n20\nWhich of the following is typical of the clinical presentation of migraine headache?\nThe majority of migraineurs experience an aura that precedes or accompanies the attack.\nMigraine headaches most often occur in the early evening.\nThe pain of migraine is most often described as sharp and stabbing.\nHeadache pain is typically unilateral in location.\nWernicke\u0027s encephalopathy is attributed to\nSevere riboflavin deficiency\nExcessive blood alcohol content\nSevere thiamine deficiency\nExcessive GGT levels\nWhich is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia?\nHallucinations\nDelusions\nDisorganized behaviour\nLack of volition\nTrigger factors for migraine headache include the following EXCEPT:\nBright lights\nAlcohol\nInsufficient sleep\nExcitement\nDivalproex sodium is associated with\nHaving better antidepressant effects compared with antimanic effects.\nCausing a 1 to 5 percent risk of neural tube birth defects if given during the first trimester of pregnancy.\nRequiring a very slow titration schedule over several weeks in order to tolerate its adverse effects.\nCausing a suppression of thyroid functioning during therapy.\nWhich of the following would be considered reasonable first line therapy for a patient with severe migraine headaches?\nAcetaminophen/aspirin/caffeine\nAcetaminophen/oxycodone\nAspirin with metoclopramide\nZolmitriptan\nThe drug of choice in the management of alcohol withdrawal is\nBarbiturates\nBenzodiazepines\nDisulfiram\nAcamprosate\nSchizophrenia is usually diagnosed in:\nInfancy\nChildhood\nEearly adulthood\nOld age\nSeeing mermaids would be a _______. Believing you are transforming into a mermaid would be a _______.\nHallucination; delusion\nDelusion; hallucination\nPositive symptom; negative symptom\nNegative symptom; positive symptom\nThe most appropriate plan for discontinuation of lorazepam (4 mg.daily) in a patient who has taken this agent for the past 6 months is which of the following?\nSwitch to an equivalent dose of buspirone and reduce the dose by 25 percent every week.\nAdd imipramine for 1 month and then taper both drugs over 2 weeks.\nTaper the daily dose of lorazepam by 25 percent every week.\nReduce the dose to 3 mg for a week, then to 2 mg for a week, and then by 12.5 percent each week.\nFN, an 83 year old female, has early onset Alzheimer\u0027s disease, Parkinson\u0027s disease, mild heart failure and urinary incontinence. She often becomes agitated in the evening. Her current medications include the following: Levodopa-carbidopa CR 200/50 mg po bid, Risperidone 0.25 mg po bid, Bumetanide 1 mg po daily, Donepezil 10 mg po daily, Ramipril 2.5 mg po qam, Calcium citrate 500 mg (elemental) po tid. Which of FN\u0027s medications may worsen her Parkinson\u0027s diseases?\nRisperidone\nDonepezil\nRamipril\nCalcium citrate\nWhich of the following factors explains the increase in diabetes in people with schizophrenia?\nGenetics\nHigh birth weight\nPoor nutrition\nAntipsychotic medication\nA homeless middle-aged male patient presents in the emergency department in a state of intoxication. You note that he is behaviorally disinhibited and rowdy. He tells you that he has recently consumed about a pint of a red-colored liquid that his friends were using to “get high.” He complains that his vision is blurred and that it is “like being in a snowstorm.” His breath smells a bit like formaldehyde. He is acidotic. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient’s intoxicated state?\nEthanol\nEthylene glycol\nIsopropanol\nMethanol\nA 42-year-old man with a history of alcoholism is brought to the emergency department in a confused and delirious state. He has truncal ataxia and ophthalmoplegia. The most appropriate immediate course of action is to administer diazepam plus\nChlordiazepoxide\nDisulfiram\nFolic acid\nThiamine\nA 45-year-old moderately obese man has been drinking heavily for 72 h. This level of drinking is much higher than his regular habit of drinking 1 alcoholic drink per day. His only significant medical problem is mild hypertension, which is adequately controlled by Metoprolol. With this history, this man is at significant risk for\nBacterial pneumonia\nCardiac arrhythmias\nHyperthermia\nTonic-clonic seizures\nA freshman student (weight 70 kg) attends a college party where he rapidly consumes a quantity of an alcoholic beverage that results in a blood level of 500 mg/dL. Assuming that this young man has not had an opportunity to develop tolerance to ethanol, his present condition is best characterized as\nAble to walk, but not in a straight line\nAlert and competent to drive a car\nComatose and near death\nSedated with increased reaction times\nA 23-year-old pregnant woman with alcoholism presented to the emergency department in the early stages of labor. She had consumed large amounts of alcohol throughout her pregnancy. This patient’s infant is at high risk of a syndrome that includes\nAmbiguous genitalia in male fetus and normal genitalia in a female fetus\nFailure of closure of the atrial septum or ventricular septum\nLimb or digit malformation\nMental retardation and craniofacial abnormalities\nA young male patient is brought to the emergency department suffering from an overdose of cocaine after its intravenous administration. His symptoms are not likely to include\nAgitation\nBradycardia\nHyperthermia\nSeizures\nRegular low-to-moderate alcohol consumption is associated with:\nLower incidence of coronary artery disease\nMyocardial depression\nPhysical dependence\nWernicke\u0027s encephalopathy\nEffect of alcohol on sleep has the following feature:\nIt is a dependable hypnotic but is not prescribed because of abuse potential\nIt consistently improves the quality of sleep\nIt can disorganize sleep architecture\nIt suppresses sleep apnea\nEthanol is used in methanol poisoning because it:\nAntagonizes the actions of methanol\nStimulates the metabolism of methanol and reduces its blood level\nInhibits the metabolism of methanol and generation of toxic metabolite\nReplenishes the folate stores depleted by methanol\nThe preferred drug for suppressing febrile convulsions is:\nIntramuscular phenobarbitone\nIntravenous phenytoin\nRectal diazepam\nOral sodium valproate\nA combination of two or more antiepileptic drugs is used:\nRoutinely in all types of epilepsy\nIn all cases of complex partial seizures\nIn all cases of secondarily generalized seizures\nOnly when monotherapy with first/second choice drugs fails\nAn epileptic woman controlled by phenytoin therapy conceives. Which of the following measures is most appropriate:\nMedical termination of pregnancy\nWithdraw phenytoin therapy\nGradually reduce phenytoin dose to the lowest effective level\nSubstitute phenytoin with a combination of carbamazepine and sodium valproate\nBradykinesia has made drug treatment necessary in a 60-year-old male patient with Parkinson’s disease, and therapy is to be initiated with levodopa. The dispensing pharmacist will (or should) know that levodopa\nCauses fewer CNS side effects if given together with a drug that inhibits hepatic dopa decarboxylase\nFluctuates in its effectiveness with increasing frequency as treatment continues\nPrevents extrapyramidal adverse effects of antipsychotic drugs\nProtects against cancer in patients with melanoma\nRisk of neural tube defect in the offspring can be minimized in pregnant women receiving antiepileptic drugs by supplemental therapy with:\nFolic acid\nVitamin A\nVitamin E\nPyridoxine\nIn parkinsonian patients, levodopa exerts the following effects except:\nReduces skeletal muscle contractility\nDecreases muscle tone\nIncreases locomotor activity\nInhibits muscle tremor\nUse of drugs of abuse is a known risk factor for the development of seizures, especially when associated with\nInfection.\nHypoglycemia.\nTuberous sclerosis.\nOverdose and withdrawal.\nAn aura usually lasts for\nAfew seconds.\nSeveral hours.\nSeveral days.\nWeeks or months.\nWhich of the following antiepileptic drugs is known to increase the risk of neural tube defects in the developing fetus?\nValproate\nPhenobarbital\nGabapentin\nLamotrigine\nA 30-year-old male patient is on drug therapy for a psychiatric problem. He complains that he feels “flat” and that he gets confused at times. He has been gaining weight and has lost his sex drive. As he moves his hands, you notice a slight tremor. He tells you that since he has been on medication he is always thirsty and frequently has to urinate. The drug he is most likely to be taking is\nCarbamazepine\nClozapine\nLithium\nRisperidone\nLong-term marijuana use may result in __________, even long after one stops using.\nVisual processing difficulties.\nShort-term memory impairment.\nDecreased attention span.\nIncreased agitation\nWhich of the following symptoms is not characteristic during the course of illness in a patient with a diagnosis of probable Alzheimer\u0027s disease?\nDelirium\nAnomia\nWandering\nParanoid delusions\nWhich of the following drugs is established to be both effective and safe to use in a pregnant patient suffering from bipolar disorder?\nCarbamazepine\nValproic acid\nLithium\t\nOlanzapine\nWhich of the following drugs is most likely to be of value in obsessive-compulsive disorders?\nAmitriptyline\nBupropion\nClomipramine\nTrazodone\nA 40-year-old woman has sporadic attacks of intense anxiety with marked physical symptoms, including hyperventilation, tachycardia, and sweating. If she is diagnosed as suffering from a panic disorder, the most appropriate drug to use is\nAlprazolam\nEszopiclone\nFlurazepam\nPropranolol\nWhich among the following would be the preferred treatment option for a 36-year-old woman with PSYCHOTIC DEPRESSION? \nImipramine plus risperidone\nImipramine plus sertraline\nTranylcypromine\nFluoxetine\nAccording to DSM-IV-TR, criteria of major depression include\nSymptoms present for at least 1 year\nan inability to work\nan increased focus and ability to concentrate\nfeelings of inappropriate guilt\nWhen choosing patient-specific antidepressant therapy, which of the following should be considered?\nPast history of response or intolerance\nAll of the statements are true \nConcomitant medications\nCost of the medication regimen\nAn acutely manic patient with significant psychotic features is started on lithium and risperidone. The best short-term adjunctive treatment to reduce agitation and cause sedation in this patient would be to add\nGabapentin.\nPropranolol.\nClonazepam.\nClonidine.\nPatients receiving mood stabilizers should be monitored for all the following EXCEPT?\nDecreased white blood counts with carbamazepine and clozapine.\nWeight gain with olanzapine, lithium, and valproate.\nHypothyroidism with lithium.\nPolycystic ovaries and menstrual irregularities with gabapentin.\nThe most important point of counseling for a patient with panic disorder receiving an initial prescription for alprazolam 0.5 mg three times daily is which of the following?\nDo not discontinue the alprazolam abruptly.\nConcurrent use of alcohol is permissible.\nAfter several weeks, take the full dose at bedtime.\nThere are no drug interactions with this medication.\nComplications during a seizure can include all of the following, EXCEPT:\nBurns and bruises\nPneumonia and sinusitis\nAbrasions and head injuries\nTongue bite and broken bones\nA patient who presents with a history of anxiety symptoms consisting of excessive worry, muscle tension, fatigue, and irritability lasting for the past 6 months that interferes with occupational and personal relationships is experiencing which of the following?\nAcute stress disorder\nGeneralized anxiety disorder\nPosttraumatic stress disorder\nPanic disorder\nA 19-year-old woman is experiencing repeated episodes of uncontrolled twitches and jerks of her right hand. She is alert throughout these episodes. Her neurological examination and laboratory tests are normal. An EEG reveals left temporal spikes consistent with seizures. What type of seizure is she most likely experiencing?\nComplex partial\nMyoclonic\nInfantile spasms\nSimple partial\nA 33-year-old man with known epilepsy is seen in the emergency department after a seizure with an associated fall. IV access is obtained. He is initially at his baseline neurologic status but then develops generalized seizure activity that has lasted 5 minutes. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\nBuccal midazolam\nIV diazepam\nIV fosphenytoin\nIV lorazepam\nWhich of the following status epilepticus etiologies would be present more commonly in pediatric patients compared with adult patients?\nLow antiepileptic drug concentrations\nInfection\nCerebrovascular accident\nCentral nervous system tumors\nWhich of the following is an accepted first-line therapy for severe head injury in a patient with congestive heart failure or pulmonary edema?\nMannitol\nHypothermia\nPentobarbital\nFurosemide\nWhich of the following is typical of the clinical presentation of cluster headache?\nCluster headaches most often occur in the morning.\nCluster periods are followed by pain-free intervals in the majority of patients.\nHeadache pain develops gradually and peaks slowly.\nCluster headache sufferers typically experience an aura that precedes or accompanies the attack.\nA 68-year-oldChinese male presents to the emergency department with a right facial droop that spares his nano-labial folds with associated speech changes. He makes a spontaneous recovery within 4 minutes. His blood pressure is 168/98. His LDL cholesterol is 1.75, with total cholesterol 4.3. A CT-B and CT-A are normal, as are MRI diffusion weighted images. An EEG shows a normal background posterior rhythm. Hotter monitor did not show any evidence of AF. What is the most appropriate management for this patient?\nClopidogrel + statin + ace-inhibitor + no-driving for 2 weeks\nAspirin + dipyridamole + statin + ace-inhibitor + no-driving for 2 weeks\nAspirin + Clopidogrel + statin + ace-inhibitor + no-driving for 2 weeks\nAspirin + Clopidogrel + ace-inhibitor + no-driving for 2 weeks\nA 72-year-old man presents to the emergency department after experiencing a 10-minute episode of slurred speech and left facial droop. He has a history of diabetes, hypertension, gout, hyperlipidemia, and alcoholism. He is an active cigarette smoker with a 25 pack-year history. His current blood pressure is 156/79 mm Hg. Neurologic examination at the time of arrival to the emergency department is normal. His low-density lipoprotein cholesterol level is 145 mg/dL. Which of the following is the strongest risk factor for stroke in this patient?\nAlcoholism\nDiabetes\nHyperlipidemia\nHypertension\nA 65-year-old right-handed woman developed a sudden inability to speak properly while she was talking to her neighbour. Her husband brought her to hospital immediately and described the problem as if she were talking “double Dutch”. Her past medical history comprised hypertension and osteoarthritis. She was taking bendroflumethiazide 2.5mg od and prn codeine. On examination, there were obvious word-finding difficulties and problems correctly naming objects. Understanding appeared to be intact. Her blood pressure was 190/100 mmHg. There was no other abnormality on examination. What is the next best step in management? \nAmlodipine 5mg orally \nAlteplase 0.9 mg/kg intravenously \nAspirin 300mg orally PLUS Clopidogrel 300mg orally \nTenecteplase 50mg intravenously\nSECTION B: ANSWER ALL QUESTIONS                                                                        (20 MARKS)\nElaborate four (4)non-motor symptoms of Parkinson’s disease               (4 Marks)\nExplain four(4) differences between tension type headaches and migraine headaches          \n                                                                                                                   (4 Marks)\nDescribe Four (4) strategies used in the management of raised ICP associated with Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI)                                                           (4 Marks)\nElucidate four (4) modifiable risk factors for ischaemic stroke                (4 Marks)\nIdentify Four(4) acute effects of marijuana                                             (4 Marks)\nSECTION C: ANSWER ALL THE  QUESTIONS                                                            (20 MARKS)\nEC is a 35-year-old female who was first diagnosed as having epilepsy at age 17. His seizures have never been controlled on medication and he now present to you suffering from an apparent generalized motor seizure. Her husband states that unlike previous seizures which have lasted approximately 5 minutes, this episode began 20 minutes ago and has not stopped.\n(a) Elaborate the acute management of this patient                             (6 Marks)\n(b)  Elucidate four (4) principles of chronic management of EC’s condition assuming she was pregnant                                                                 (4 Marks)\nJ.S. a 55-year-old 5’6” tall 100-kg man experienced a rapidly progressive paralysis of his right arm and slurred speech yesterday. The symptoms lasted for 15 to 20 minutes and resolved rapidly. His neurological examination is entirely normal, and he denies any feeling of weakness. He smokes 2 packs of cigarettes daily and drinks 3 to 6 bottles of beer each evening. His physical examination is entirely normal, except for the carotid bruit, which was noted 2 years ago. His blood pressure (BP) is 165/100mmHg, hemoglobin (Hb) is 17 (Normal 12-16), hematocrit is 55 % (normal 42 %-52 %), total serum cholesterol 300mg/dL (Normal \u003c200mg/dL). His HbA1C is 10 %. His Doppler ultrasound of carotid shows 90 % stenosis on the left carotid and 40 % stenosis on the right carotid. A quick diagnosis of transient ischaemic attack (TIA) is made.\nIllustrate the data in J.S. history that is consistent with TIA?                       \n                                                                                         (5points @ 1mark)\n(b) Identify the initial and long-term goals in treating J.S?                     (3 Marks)\n(c) Elaborate how the risk factors for J.S can be modified to prevent further attack of TIA                                                                                                     (6 Marks)","exam-infections-i":"KABARAK                         UNIVERSITY\nUNIVERSITY EXAMINATIONS\nMAIN CAMPUS\nFIRST SEMESTER, 2020/2021 ACADEMIC YEAR\nEXAMINATION FOR BACHELOR DEGREE IN PHARMACY\nPHAM 4219: CLINICAL PHARMACY V (INFECTIONS I)\nSTREAM: Y4S2\t\t\t\t\t\t\t   TIME: 9:00-11:00AM\nEXAMINATION SESSION: SEPT-DEC \t   \t\t    DATE: 12/02/2021\nREAD INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY\nThe examination paper has THREE (3) sections.                                  [Total 100 Marks]\nAnswer ALL questions in sections A \u0026 B and ONLY ONE in C\nSECTION A has SIXTY Multiple Choice Questions.                                        [60 Marks]\nSECTION B has FIVE Short Answer Questions.                                               [20 Marks]\nSECTION C has TWO Long Answer Questions.                                               [20 Marks]\nAnswer questions in the BOOKLET provided. Begin each of the questions in a new page of the answer booklet. \nAdhere to the University examination rules and regulations\nDo not write anything on the question paper.\nSECTION A: ANSWER ALL THE QUESTIONS                                                (60 MARKS)\nA 30-year-old man presents with diplopia, dysphagia and dysarthria. After 12 hours he has weakness of his arms but remains afebrile and is not confused. Over the next 12 hours he develops respiratory failure and requires artificial ventilation. What is the likely diagnosis?\nTetanus\nDiphtheria\nBotulism\nStrychnine poisoning\nA 21-year-old student develops abdominal pain, fever and diarrhoea 12 hours after eating a chicken sandwich that had been in his fridge for a week. The most likely cause is: \nCampylobacter\nNorwalk virus\nSalmonella\nE. coli O157\nAcute osteomyelitis is commonly caused by:\nStaph aureus\nS. pyogenes \nH. influenzae\nSalmonella\n An 18-month-old boy presented with 4 days of sudden onset, severe watery diarrhea, after 2 days of being non specifically unwell. He was passing stool approximately 15 times/day. He had vomited for one day, but this then stopped. There was no blood in the stool. He was also noted to have a dry cough and appeared to have coryzal symptoms. He had not had a wet nappy for 24 hours. He had stopped breastfeeding at 12 months old. On examination, he was markedly dehydrated, with a temperature of 38.5 °C and a tachycardia. He required aggressive fluid resuscitation. In addition to intravenous fluids or oral rehydration solutions, what agent would improve his outcome?\nAscorbic acid\nCholecalciferol\nThiamine\nZinc\nA 21-year-old woman presented to the sexual health clinic with multiple painless lesions over her vulva and perianal area. These were skin-colored, mostly smooth papules of \u003c5 mm, but with a few raised cauliflower-shaped clusters.What human papillomavirus (HPV) type is most likely to have caused this infection?\nHPV-1\nHPV-6\nHPV-16\nHPV-18\nA 29-year-old man seeks medical attention on returning from a camping holiday in Amboseli National Park. He sustained a bite wound to his left hand 4 days previously from a wild dog he had tried to feed by hand. He has not seen any other healthcare professionals. He is concerned about the risk of rabies. He has received a full course of tetanus vaccinations and the wound looks clean and dry. Identify the most appropriate intervention that is required?\nWound debridement and washout only\nFive doses of IM rabies vaccine days 0, 3, 7, 14 and 28 only\nFive doses of IM rabies vaccine days 0, 3, 7, 14 and 28 plus Human rabies immunoglobulin infiltrated into the wound\nHuman rabies immunoglobulin infiltrated into the wound only\nA 32-year-old woman presented with a 7-day history of cramping abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea. She had opened her bowels over 15 times in the preceding 24 hours. There was fresh red blood mixed in with the stool. She had vomited once, and felt feverish. Her symptoms had not abated since their onset. She had returned from a family holiday with her partner and three children 2 days before onset. Nobody else was symptomatic. She had no past medical history of note. \nStool culture was positive for Campylobacter jejuni\nIn addition to fluid replacement therapy, what is the most appropriate management?\nAzithromycin\nCiprofloxacin\nMetronidazole\nNo antimicrobials needed\nAn 8-year-old boy was admitted to a hospital in Nakuru with torrential rice water diarrhea. He was from a rural area where there had been a recent outbreak of cholera associated with contaminated drinking water supply.\nWhat is the mechanism of action of the cholera toxin?\nADP ribosylation of Elongation Factor 2\nADP ribosylation of inhibitory G proteins leading to reduced cAMP\nADP ribosylation of stimulatory G proteins leading to elevated intracellular levels of cAMP\nFormation of cation-selective pores in cell membranes\nA 34-year-old woman presented with migratory swellings over her right arm and back. They typically lasted for 1–3 days, and were hot, red, painful and itchy. She had returned 4 weeks earlier from a 12-month job in Cameroon. A presumptive diagnosis of loiasis was made, pending investigations.\nWhat was the most likely vector for her infection?\nAnopheles mosquito\nChrysops deerfly\nCulex mosquito\nMansonia mosquito\nA 12-year-old male child with sickle cell disease is brought to a clinic with complaints of fever and right knee pain. The client has severe pain and tenderness in the tibia just below the right knee. An evaluation reveals acute osteomyelitis. What would the nurse comprehend as the most likely organism responsible for the osteomyelitis in the client?\nStaphylococcus\nStreptococcus\nSalmonella\nPseudomonas \n A 4-year-old male has recently recovered from chicken pox. He now refuses to walk. He has been complaining of pain worsening over the past 3 or 4 days. The patient has had tactile fevers. There has been no known trauma. An exam is remarkable for a temperature of 38.3C, pain to palpation of the right knee, and increased warmth of the right distal thigh. There are no joint effusions and range of motion is normal. Which of the following bacteria is most likely responsible for this illness?\nSalmonella\nStaphylococcus aureus\nGroup A Streptococcus\nHaemophilus influenzae \n A 9-year-old boy was placed in a short leg splint in an emergency department after twisting his ankle during dancing practice. He presents 10 days later with increasing pain and fevers up to 390C over the last 3 days. The splint is removed revealing intact skin integrity with notable swelling and erythema overlying the distal fibula. Lab results include a C-reactive protein level of 12mg/L (normal 0-3.2 mg/L), erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 38mm/h (normal 0-20mm/h) and a white blood cell count of 12.3 K/mm3(normal 4.3 -11.4 K/mm3). A diagnosis of distal fibula osteomyelitis is made. What would be the most appropriate next step in the management of this patient? \nIrrigation and debridement of distal fibular osteomyelitis\nPlace in short leg walking cast for 3 weeks\nBegin oral antibiotics and follow up in 2 weeks\nPerform stress radiographs to assess integrity of the syndesmosis\nA 9-year-old boy is being treated for acute hematogenous osteomyelitis of the distal tibia with appropriate IV antibiotic therapy. After three days of treatment, he fails to show any clinical improvement. Advanced imaging is obtained and reveals a 1.5x1.5cm abscess in the distal tibia. The patient subsequently undergoes formal open surgical debridement, without complications. Following surgery, serial evaluations of which of the following studies is the most expeditious(reliable) method to determine the early success of treatment?  \nWhite blood cell count\nMRI\nErythrocyte sedimentation rate\nC-reactive protein\nA 25-year old man presents to the Accident and Emergency Department complaining of fever, right knee pain and rigors. He has no previous relevant medical history. He recalls recently hurting his knee during a soccer match. The knee is hot and swollen on physical examination. A presumptive diagnosis of septic arthritis is made.\nThe most likely cause is\nNeisseria gonorrhoea\nEscherichia coli\nParvovirus\nStaphylococcus aureus\nWhich combination of investigations is most likely to confirm the cause?\nBlood cultures, joint aspirate and radiology\nFull blood count and radiology\nCT scan and blood cultures\nIndium labeled bone scan, and blood cultures\nA 22-year-old previously healthy university student is evaluated for fever, joint pain, and rash for last 48 hours.  She denies any preceding infections like diarrhea, urethral discharge, or sore throat. There is no history of travel, tick bite, or prior joint symptoms. She is sexually active, and the last menstrual period a week ago. On examination, the temperature is 38.8oC and had a petechial skin rash with pustules over her arms.  There is no erythroderma.  There is tenosynovitis in flexor tendons of the wrist. \nInvestigations: \nHb 13.6g/l,  \nWBC count 13.4,   \nNeutrophils 7.6,  \nplatelets 437,  \nANA negative, \nrenal and liver function tests normal. \nCRP 318 and \nESR 96. \nWhat should be your working diagnosis? \nGonococcal arthritis \nMeningococcal septicaemia \nPustular psoriasis\nReactive arthritis\n  Select the Commonest organism causing osteomyelitis in children under 3 years: \nHaemophilus\nStaphylococcal\nStreptococcal\nSalmonella\n Which of the follwing statements is CORRECT regarding V. cholerae infection?\nThe organism is isolated from a blood sample.\nThe natural reservoir of the organism is domestic animals.\nThe LPS complex is the main virulence factor.\nAn enterotoxin is the main virulence factor.\n Each of the following agents is a recognized cause of diarrhoea EXCEPT:\nClostridium perfringens\nEnterocooccus faecalis\nShigellaa dysenteriae\nVibrio vholerae\nCampylobacter jejuni\n A 7-year-old girl was ready to be discharged after a 2-day course of vancomycin therapy for the treatment of a carbuncle in her right buttock. The culture from the abscess grew MRSA sensitive to all antibiotics tested except erythromycin and oxacillin, and the double disk diffusion test was positive. Which one of the following would best complete the course of antibiotic therapy for this patient?\nClindamycin\nDoxycycline\nLevofloxacin\nLinezolid\nYou are asked to implement an antimicrobial stewardship initiative to decrease the incidence of Clostridium difficile infection (CDI) after several patients who were treated with clindamycin for skin and soft tissue infections (SSTIs) came back to the emergency department with CDI. Physicians in your department have recently switched from cephalexin to clindamycin because of the sharp increase in the incidence of community-associated MRSA (CAMRSA) infections. In addition to incision and drainage, which one of the following would be best to recommend for the treatment of moderate purulent SSTIs?\nContinue the use of clindamycin if the double disk diffusion test is positive\nUse cephalexin instead of clindamycin\nUse linezolid instead of clindamycin\nUse trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole instead of clindamycin\n  A 65-year-old man was bitten in his leg by a dog while walking in the park. His wounds are infected. He has had uncontrolled type 2 diabetes mellitus for the past 5 years, for which he takes metformin 1000 mg PO twice daily. He developed hives when he received sulfa drugs. He received the full primary vaccine series of tetanus immunization and had a tetanus toxoid-containing booster 12 years ago. In addition to cleansing and irrigation, which one of the following is the most appropriate treatment for this patient?\nAmoxicillin-clavulanate\nAmoxicillin-clavulanate plus a tetanus toxoid containing vaccine\nCiprofloxacin plus clindamycin\nMoxifloxacin plus a tetanus toxoid-containing vaccine\nA man sustains a human bite wound to the hand during an altercation. The most appropriate initial course of action is to aggressively clean the wound and\nWithhold antimicrobial therapy until signs of infection are present\nAdminister penicillin 500 mg PO every 6 hours plus dicloxacillin 500 mg PO every 6 hours for 5 days as prophylactic therapy\nAdminister cefaclor 500 mg PO every 8 hours for 5 days as prophylactic therapy\nAdminister cephalexin 500 mg PO every 6 hours as empirical therapy\nThe skin is very red, swollen and easy to demarcate, and the most common cause of it is Streptococci Group A; all these are features of:\nErysipelas \nFuruncles\nCellulitis\nSteven Johnson syndrome\n Complication of amoebic dysentery includes all the following except:\nAmeboma\nIntestinal perforation and peritonitis\nExtra intestinal lesions\nPeptic ulcer disease\n All the following are True regarding acute osteomyelitis in a child EXCEPT?\nDiagnosis by X-ray is 8-10 days after onset\nThere is diffuse tenderness at the site\nAntibiotic therapy should be at least for 4 weeks \nSalmonella is the most common cause\nWhich among the following is not a Complication of acute osteomyelitis?\nMalignancy\nFracture of the affected bone\nSepsis\nChronicity\n Most common cause of nongonococcal septic arthritis is -\nStaph-aureus\nH.influenza\nPseudomonas\nStreptococcus\n Pseudomembranous colitis is caused\nClostridium tetani\nClostridium difficile\nClostridium botulinum\nClostridium perfringens\n A 4-year-old boy presents to his pediatrician with intense perianal itching. His mother explains that the child has also been extremely irritable during the day and has not been sleeping well at night. Eggs with a flattened side were identified by the laboratory technician from a piece of scotch tape brought in by the parent. Infection with which of the following organisms is most likely?\nEnterobius vermicularis\nAscaris lumbricoides\nEntamoeba histolytica\nTrichuris trichiura\n Pigs or dogs are the source of human infection by each of the following parasites EXCEPT:\nEchinococcus granulosus\nTrichinella spiralis\nAscaris lumbricoides\nTaenia solium\nWhich of the following is most likely to be a source of food poisoning bacteria?\nFrozen peas\nPasteurised milk\nTinned salmon\nRaw meat  \n  Which one of the following bacteria causes the greatest number of cases of food poisoning?\nClostridium perfringens\nListeria\nStaphylococcus aureus\nSalmonella\n30. The main symptom of Staphylococcus food poisoning is:\nvomiting\ndiarrhoea\nfever\nabdominal pains\nEscherichia coli is one of the major enteric bacteria that causes diarrhea and is characterized according to its virulence properties. All are the types of E. coli, EXCEPT?\nEnterohemorrhagic E. coli\nEnteropathogenic E. coli\nEnterotoxigenic E. coli\nEnterolysogenic E. coli \n The symptoms of typhoid fever develop in one to three weeks after exposure to S. typhi, all of the given below are the major symptoms, EXCEPT?\nWeight gain\nHeadache\nHigh-grade fever\nRashes\nOne of the most common food-borne illnesses from undercooked poultry and eggs is:\nTrichinella\nSalmonella\nClostridium perfringens\nClostridium botulinum \n A patient determined to be severely dehydrated based on weight loss and clinical signs and symptoms should be rehydrated with\nOral rehydration solution\nIntravenous normal saline\nIntravenous D5W\nIntravenous colloid solutions\nWhich of the following is true with regard to oral rehydration therapy?\nRequires hospitalization to administer\nCannot be used in infants with infrequent vomiting\nOften ineffective\nInexpensive\nWhich is not an advantage of low-osmolarity (rice-based) ORT over isotonic, traditional ORT?\nMore effectively replenishes electrolytes\nDecreases stool frequency\nDecreases duration of diarrhea\nDecreases stool volume\nWhich of the following diarrhea categories can be treated safely with antimotility agents?\nEmpirical traveler\u0027s diarrhea (nonbloody)\nDysentery\nEHEC\nShigellosis\nAn 8-year-old with cholera should be treated with\nCiprofloxacin\nDoxycycline\nTMP-SMX\nAntibiotic use is not indicated\n Some of the more common causes of food poisoning include all these except\nShigella\nSalmonella\nS. aureus\nV. cholerae\n All the following are major complications of falciparum malaria infection Except\nSeizures and coma.\nHypoglycemia.\nSevere anaemia\nPeritonitis\n The drug of choice for the eradication of lice infestation (body, head, and crab lice) is\n1% menthol\nPermethrin 1%\nY-benzene hexachloride\nSulfur ointment\nWhich of the following agents should be avoided in patients with glucose-6-PD deficiency?\nIvermectin\nMefloquine\nPrimaquine\nAlbendazole\n Black water fever is a special manifestation of malaria caused by;\nP. falciparum\nP. malariae\nP. ovale\nP. vivax\nAll of the following statements regarding Plasmodium falciparum are true EXCEPT?\nCauses more severe disease in pregnancy\nIs associated with recurrent relapses after initial treatment because of liver hypnozoites\nIs the only malarial parasite causing greater than 20% parasitaemia\nIs the only cause of cerebral malaria \nWhich of the following statements is true regarding amoebic liver abscess?\nShould be treated by diloxanide furoate alone\nShould be aspirated routinely\nIs associated with eosinophilia\nShould be treated using a combination of metronidazole and diloxanide\nSubcutaneous nodules are a typical finding in all the following infections except?\nNeurofibromatosis\nHydatid disease\nCysticercosis\nOnchocerciasis\nAll the following are characteristic features of acute schistosomiasis EXCEPT?\nFever\nEosinophilia\nMyocarditis\nIncubation period of 4-6 weeks\nEffective malaria control interventions include all the following EXCEPT?\nMass use of Fansidar chemoprophylaxis\nUse of pyrethroid impregnated mosquito nets\nLarviciding\nEradication of mosquito breeding sites  \nAll the following occur in Chagas\u0027 disease (American trypanosomiasis) EXCEPT?\nLymphadenopathy\nMeningoencephalitis\nCalabar swelling\nMega-oesophagus\nBlindness is a recognised complication of all the following infections EXCEPT?\nOnchocerciasis\nAmoebiasis \nCysticercosis\nToxoplasmosis\nWhich of the following may not present with fever and diarrhoea?\nMalaria\nGiardiasis\nCampylobacter enteritis\nBrucellosis  \nSteroids are of benefit in the treatment of\nVisceral leishmaniasis\nEschericia coli septicaemia\nCerebral malaria\nSevere typhoid fever \n Which of the following parasites can cause rectal prolapse?\nHookworm\nAscaris\nTrichuris\nAll of the above\nA 12-year-old patient (no significant Past Medical History) with salmonella enterocolitis with moderate symptoms should receive which of the following regimens?\nORT + ciprofloxacin + loperamide\nORT + ciprofloxacin\nORT + azithromycin\nORT\nIntervention strategies for malaria include:\nInsecticide-treated bed net\nIndoor residual spraying with DDT\nRemoval of mosquito breeding sites\nAll of the above  \nTreatment of acute diarrhea includes:\nOral rehydration and penicillin\nOral rehydration, zinc supplementation\nIntravenous saline and penicillin\nOral rehydration, energy-rich food, and zinc supplementation \nSECTION B: SHORT ANSWER ALL THE QUESTIONS                          (20 MARKS)\nA 17-year-old boy who plays football has a sudden onset of fever, fatigue, as well as pain in his right lower leg. He is no longer able to walk because of the pain and appears to be acutely ill. His temperature is 103.3°F. Upon examination he has limited movement of his right leg, and there is swelling and point tenderness over the proximal, medial lower leg. The skin around the tenderness seems intact and there is no sign of trauma. His labs are as follows:\nHemoglobin: 12.5 g/dL\nLeukocyte count: 15,000/mm³**\nESR: 65 mm/h**\nA technetium bone scan is conducted and shows increased uptake at the metaphysics of the tibia. \nIdentify the most likely diagnosis.                                                            (2 Marks)\nOutline the pharmacological and non-pharmacological management      (4 Marks) \nIdentify the most important member of Enterobacteriaceae that\ncauses each of the following diseases:                                                                  (4 Marks)\nChildhood diarrhea\nTraveler\u0027s diarrhoea\nBacillary dysentery\nHemorrhagic colitis or Hemolytic Uremic syndrome (HUS)\nIdentify 3 human worms (parasites) that can cause iron deficiency anaemia and 1 parasite associated with pernicious anaemia                                                                      (4 Marks)\nPablo, a 41-year old security officer is bitten by a neighbor’s dog while passing near the neighbor’s drive way. He calls you for advice regarding the next course of action. You establish that the vaccination history for the dog is unclear. Formulate an appropriate treatment plan for Pablo and include the counselling points                                (4 Marks)\nMatch each clinical presentation with the causative parasite                               (2 Marks)\nSECTION C: LONG ESSAY QUESTIONS                                                          (20 MARKS)\nB.C. is a 49-year-old obese woman, presenting to the emergency department with a 1-week history of fever, confusion and delirium, abdominal pain, headache, anorexia, and diarrhea. One day before admission, she noted a new, red rash on her chest. Her history of present illness is significant for returning 10 days ago from travel to Mathare slums, where she stayed with relatives, some of whom were recovering from typhoid fever. B.C.’s medical history is significant for gallstones. She lives in California with her husband. Admission vital signs are as follows: temperature is 38.30C and heart rate is 60 beats/minute; blood pressure is stable. Physical examination is significant for splenomegaly and hepatomegaly. Laboratory tests include the following: WBC is 3.0 × 106/μL, liver function tests are mildly elevated, and the results of two sets of blood cultures are pending. B.C. \nIdentify the most likely presumptive diagnosis and the probable causative agent the infection is attributable to                                                                 (2 Marks) \nWhy is her history of present illness, clinical presentation, and laboratory results consistent with this diagnosis?                                      (4 points @1 Mark each)\nIllustrate three benefits of antimicrobial treatment in BC                     (3 Marks)\nDesign an appropriate treatment plan for BC (include adjunctive and supportive therapy)                                                                                                 (5 Marks)\nFourteen months after discharge from the hospital, B.C.’s stool is still positive for the same microbial agent. Propose 4 therapeutic options that are available to cure B.C.’s chronic carrier state?                                                                     (4 Marks)\nOutline 2 preventive measures against B. C’s condition                          (2 Marks)\nA boy aged 5 years from SECTION 58 is brought to your hospital’s outpatient department. The mother says he was well until this morning when he woke up and said he was feeling tired and vomited after taking his breakfast. When the mother touched him he felt hot and she gave him a half tablet of paracetamol. When you examine, you find a well-nourished 20kg child, alert, not pale, and with axillary temperature of 38.5°C. Social history reveals that they had recently travelled to Mwea to attend a family function. The rest of the physical examination is normal:\nIdentify the most likely diagnosis and causative agent.                         (2 Marks)\nJustify your answer in (a) above                                            (4 points @0.5 each)\nPropose 3 diagnostic tests that would help you confirm your diagnosis and elaborate on their utility                                                                           (3 Marks)\nCompile an elaborate treatment plan for this patient (include the dosing regimen for all the pharmacological agents)                                                           (4 Marks)\n Illustrate 3 counselling points that you would offer the mother regarding treatment                                                                                                   (3 Marks)\nThe patient is brought back later in the night. The mother reports that the boy has not been retaining anything. He has refused food, but has drunk a little. The mother says the child had a “fit” late afternoon; he regained consciousness immediately. For the past few hours the child has been increasingly drowsy, and for the last hour has been unconscious. At the examination the child is well-nourished, unconscious, and not dehydrated. The axillary temperature is 40.2°C; pulse rate 120s/min, regular; blood pressure 90/70mmHg. There is no neck stiffness and no rash. The pupils are equal; a few retinal haemorrhages seen; no papilloedema. Some yellowish sticky fluid is seen filling the left external auditory meatus. Reflexes are normal.\nIdentify the most probable diagnosis and justify your answer                (2 Marks) \n Design an appropriate treatment plan for this patient                             (4 Marks)"};

